// nparse.n — the M5 chain, token-native end to end: a complete toy
//   compiler with FUNCTIONS, REAL IDENTIFIERS, and — since the selfhost
//   road's rung 3 — a real AST for its expressions, with a real CHECK
//   PASS and a real TRANSFORM over it. lex → parse → check → fold →
//   emit → run, in N's own syntax:
//       fn fact(n) { r := 1; if n > 1 { r := n * fact(n - 1); } r } fact(5)
//   The lexer fills a token buffer (sbrk'd i64 arrays, v0.16 index
//   stores); the parser consumes TOKENS, never characters; the VM runs
//   the emitted stack code. The grammar covers the whole toy language:
//     program := { fndef } { stmt } cmp  |  { fndef }   (rung 6: a
//                fn named main and no tail — the program IS main)
//     fndef   := 'fn' name '(' name { ',' name } ')' '{' { stmt } cmp '}'
//     stmt    := name ':=' cmp ';'
//              | 'if' cmp block [ 'else' ( block | if-stmt ) ]
//              | 'while' cmp block
//              | 'print' cmp ';'      (strings are factors now)
//              | name '(' args ')' ';'   (a call statement)
//              | 'mut' name ':=' cmp ';'  (a mutable binding)
//              | name '=' cmp ';'         (assignment: mut only)
//   `else if` CHAINS are free structure, not new machinery: the else
//   slot always held a statement index, and a nested if IS a
//   statement — the parser just recurses into ast_stmt instead of
//   demanding braces, and no walker changed at all.
//
//   THE WHOLE PROGRAM IS A TREE NOW. The old parser emitted code while
//   it descended — postfix fell out of parse order, which works
//   exactly as long as nothing needs to happen between understanding
//   the program and generating its code. A checker is precisely that
//   kind of thing: it must look at the program BEFORE code for it
//   exists, which is why ncc parses to Expr/Stmt trees first and an
//   emit-on-the-fly toy never could. So the parse rules BUILD a
//   postorder AST — expressions since rung 3, statements too since the
//   rung after — as parallel i64 node arrays carried in one struct
//   (Ast — nk kind, nl lhs, nr rhs, nv value, and na, a flat table
//   with four slots per node: call arguments, or an if's else arm).
//     node kind 0 = number (nv = value)     1 = load (nv = var slot)
//               2 = binop (nl/nr = children, nv = ITS VM OPCODE)
//               3 = negate (nl = operand)
//               4 = call (nv = fn symbol, nl = arg count, args in na)
//               5 = bind (nv = slot, nl = rhs)
//               6 = if (nl = cond, nr = then chain, na[0] = else or -1)
//               7 = while (nl = cond, nr = body chain)
//               8 = seq (nl = statement, nr = next link or -1)
//               9 = print (nl = expr; the operand's TYPE picks the
//                   opcode)                10 = string literal (nv)
//               11 = len (nl = a str operand; the measuring builtin)
//               12 = logical not (nl = an int operand; bare '!')
//               13 = itos (nl = an interpolation hole's expression;
//                    int in, its decimal text out)
//               14 = struct literal (nv = struct id, nl = field count,
//                    na = the field-value roots — the call-node shape)
//               15 = field read (nl = base; nv holds the field SYMBOL
//                    until the check pass resolves it to a slot)
//               16 = field write (a STATEMENT: nl = value, na slot 0 =
//                    the base name's symbol, nv = field, resolved)
//               17 = match (a STATEMENT: nl = scrutinee, nr = first
//                    arm) with 18 = arm (nv = variant symbol until
//                    check resolves the TAG; nl = chain, nr = next)
//               19 = enum tag (nv = tag, nr = enum id; typed 100+e)
//   Token kinds 22 (a builtin type name - i8..u64, isize, usize,
//   addr, bool - reserved words at toy scale) and 23 ('as') serve
//   N's signatures and casts: ': i64' and '-> i64' are accepted
//   (all builtins map to the toy's ONE numeric kind), and a postfix
//   'as' consumes optional '*'s plus a type name, keeping the value
//   untouched.
//               20 = variant construction (nl = payload root or -1,
//                    nv = tag, nr = enum id; lowers to RNEW)
//               21 = extern call (nv = binding index, nl = arg
//                    count, args in na; lowers to one SYSCALL)
//               22 = call statement (nl = the call, made and then
//                    dropped — a bare write(...) is a statement)
//               23 = assignment (nv = slot, nl = rhs; STORE to a
//                    name bound mut — ncc's own write path)
//   A separate walker, emit_node, then emits by postorder recursion —
//   children first, the node's own operation last. Postfix is no
//   longer a lucky consequence of parse order; it is a property of the
//   tree, and the walker produces byte-for-byte the code the emitting
//   parser used to, so every result and word count below is unchanged:
//   the architecture moved under the outputs. The caller-save call
//   machinery (save the callee's slots in order, evaluate ALL the
//   arguments before binding any — so `gcd(b, a % b)` still reads the
//   OLD values — bind in REVERSE because pops hand back the last
//   argument first, CALL, restore in reverse with SWAP + STORE) moved
//   wholesale from the parser into the walker: parse decides WHAT (a
//   call of f with these argument trees), emit decides HOW — ncc's own
//   parse → gen split, now inside the toy. And the first dividend of
//   holding a whole call as data: the parser checks the argument count
//   against the callee's parameter count ("wrong number of
//   arguments"), a diagnostic the fused version had no natural seat
//   for. Statements joined the tree next: ast_stmt/ast_block build
//   bind/if/while nodes with blocks as seq CHAINS (a cons list in the
//   same arrays: nl = statement, nr = next), and the jump patching —
//   the if/else diamond, the while back-JMP — relocated wholesale into
//   the statement walker, emit_stmt. Only the outermost skeleton still
//   emits as it parses: a function's entry recording, its parameter
//   table, and the hop-over JMP past the fndefs — headers, not
//   meaning. Each BODY (a function's, or main's) is now one finished
//   tree, and the passes run over it in order: check, fold, emit.
//   One honest consequence: parse errors now surface before check
//   errors program-wide (a body parses completely before its passes
//   run), where the fused version interleaved them in source order.
//
//   THE CHECK PASS is the payoff the AST exists for. Between build and
//   emit, check_node walks the finished tree — it reads the program
//   and emits nothing — and rejects any load of a name that is not in
//   the BOUND SET: the enclosing function's parameters plus the names
//   bound by `:=` statements already compiled. Every node records its
//   source line (nline, the sixth Ast array), so the refusal is
//   located: "line N:C: error: unknown variable". Scope is honest and
//   conservative, and it lives entirely in the CHECK walk now: a seq
//   chain threads the bound count forward, a bind adds its name only
//   AFTER its right-hand side checks, and an if arm or while body
//   checks with the count it was given and hands back the ORIGINAL —
//   names born in a branch die with it, because the checker cannot
//   know the branch will have run (the same conservative rule ncc's
//   v0.18 own-move branch merge enforces, here in miniature). A function
//   body sees ONLY its parameters and its own bindings: the flat
//   runtime table lets bodies read anything, but that is caller-save's
//   private business, and the checker now enforces the lexical story.
//
//   THE FOLD PASS is the other thing a tree buys. fold_node walks the
//   same postorder and REWRITES: a binop whose children both folded to
//   numbers becomes a number computed at COMPILE time; a negation of a
//   number likewise; call arguments fold, calls never do. The fused
//   parser-emitter could not have done this even in principle — by the
//   time it had seen both operands, their PUSHes were already emitted;
//   rewriting history is exactly what holding the program AS DATA
//   makes possible. The folding demo compiles to 10 code words where
//   the unfolded tree emits 19. Division and modulo by a constant
//   zero are left unfolded: the compiler must not crash computing
//   what the program would — the VM owns that failure, at run time.
//   Since the statements joined the tree, the fold pass rewrites THEM
//   too: an `if` whose condition folded to a constant DISSOLVES into
//   its live arm (the node is rewritten in place into a seq wrapper
//   around that arm's chain — the dead arm is simply never emitted),
//   and a `while` over a constant false vanishes the same way; a
//   constant-TRUE while is left alone, because an infinite loop is
//   the program's right. Check runs first, so a dead arm is still
//   CHECKED — dead code must still be legal code (ncc's rule too),
//   and elimination never widens scope: a binding in a live arm still
//   dies at its brace as far as the checker is concerned.
//
//   Everything below the seam is as the earlier rungs left it.
//   Identifiers are INTERNED (a names byte-buffer plus per-symbol
//   start/len; the token's value IS the symbol index, and slots,
//   function entries and parameter tables key off it). Every token
//   records its source line AND COLUMN — packed line*1000+col into the
//   one ln array, so the token machinery and every node's nline carry
//   both for free and fail() is the single decode point (toy lines
//   stay under 1000 columns; ncc carries two fields, same idea) — and
//   the parser refuses bad input at its expect points —
//   "line N:C: error: ..." and stop, first violation wins. The column
//   names the offending token, not just its neighborhood: the demo's
//   type error points at the '+' itself. Function entries are
//   recorded BEFORE bodies compile, so
//   self-recursion resolves; a JMP patched over the fndefs starts
//   execution at the main statements.
//   Token encoding: kind 0 = number (val = its value), 1 = identifier
//   (val = its SYMBOL INDEX), 2 = operator/punct (val = its byte),
//   3 = '==', 4 = ':=', 5 = 'if', 6 = 'while', 7 = 'fn', 8 = 'else',
//   9 = end, 10 = string literal (val = STRING-TABLE index),
//   11 = 'print', 12 = '!=', 13 = '<=', 14 = '>=', 15 = 'len' (a
//   reserved word now, like print), 16/18/17 = interpolation
//   HEAD/MID/TAIL segments (val = STRING-TABLE index). Line comments
//   (// to end of line) vanish in the lexer like whitespace, and so
//   do /* block comments */, which NEST by a depth counter — N's own
//   rule; the line counter ticks through both, so diagnostics stay
//   honest around them. Numbers read N's spellings too: 0x hex
//   (lowercase a-f at toy scale) and '_' digit separators, both
//   folding and interpolating like any other constant.
//
//   FILE-DRIVEN, the selfhost optic: the toy also compiles a program
//   its binary does NOT embed — an audited #[caps] wrapper reads a
//   toy-language source file from disk (in-OS, off /mnt) and the SAME
//   pipeline lexes, parses, checks, folds, emits, and RUNS it. The
//   move that made it one pipeline: the lexer takes RAW BYTES plus a
//   length (*u8 + i64 — ncc's own SRC shape) instead of a str, so an
//   embedded demo hands over its .ptr/.len and a file buffer arrives
//   as itself; N cannot wrap a pointer back into a str (the ntokens
//   finding), and now nothing ever asks it to.
//   Opcodes: 0 HALT · 1 PUSH arg · 2 ADD · 3 SUB · 4 MUL · 5 DIV ·
//   6 MOD · 7 NEG · 8 LT · 9 GT · 10 EQ · 11 LOAD slot · 12 STORE slot
//   · 13 JZ target · 14 JMP target · 15 CALL target (pushes the return
//   address) · 16 RET (returns the top value) · 17 SWAP · 18 PRINT
//   (pop, write the number + newline) · 19 PRINTS (pop a string-table
//   index, write that entry + newline) · 20 STREQ (pop two table
//   indexes, byte-compare the spans, push 0/1) · 21 CONCAT (pop two
//   table indexes, append a fresh entry = both spans joined, push its
//   index) · 22 NEQ · 23 LE · 24 GE (the full comparison set, each a
//   dedicated pop-two-push-0/1 like LT/GT/EQ — on integers) ·
//   25 STRLEN (pop a table index, push its span's length). `len(s)`
//   completes the string toolkit — make (+), compare (==), measure —
//   typed str-in/int-out, and len of a literal folds to a number at
//   compile time. · 26 NOT (pop, push the logical flip: zero to one,
//   anything else to zero) — bare `!` as a factor, int-only, folding
//   over constants; the `!=` two-char arm eats its pair first, so a
//   lone '!' can only ever mean this. · 27 STRNEQ (STREQ's
//   byte-compare, pushing the flip) — string equality is symmetric
//   now: == and != both resolve over two strings. · 28 ITOS (pop an
//   integer, append its decimal image as a fresh table entry through
//   the shared cursor, push the entry's index) — how an interpolation
//   hole turns a number into text at run time; over a constant it
//   folds instead, writing the digits at compile time. · 29 RNEW k
//   (pop k field values into a fresh record, push its base index) ·
//   30 RGET off (replace a record index with one field's word) ·
//   31 RSET off (pop the record index, then the value; store the
//   field's word in place - how `p.x = e;` mutates). · 32 DUP ·
//   33 POP (the match machinery: the scrutinee stays put while arms
//   DUP/EQ/JZ against it; the taken arm POPs it).
//
//   ENUMS + MATCH. A tag-only enum declares like a struct (tables:
//   the name and up to four variant symbols; a variant IS its
//   index). `Shape.square` resolves AT PARSE (the enum is known) to
//   one PUSH of the tag - but its TYPE is 100+e, far from int and
//   the struct ids, so a tag never adds, prints, interpolates, or
//   crosses into a plain parameter: `match` is the ONLY eliminator.
//   match compiles to DUP/PUSH/EQ/JZ chains over the scrutinee, and
//   the LAST arm runs with no test at all - the checker PROVED
//   exhaustiveness first (every variant covered exactly once: unknown
//   variants, duplicate arms, and missing arms are located errors).
//   Tags cross calls with `fn pick(s: Shape)` annotations like
//   structs do. And == over two tags stays REFUSED on purpose:
//   ncc's own enums have no == either — match is the eliminator
//   there too, so the toy mirrors the real rule, not a convenience
//   it never had.
//   PAYLOAD variants complete the enum: `num(x)` in a declaration
//   flags the variant (the name is documentary; the payload is one
//   integer word), and an enum with ANY payload variant represents
//   ALL its values as records — tag at word 0, payload after it,
//   built by the same RNEW that builds structs (a bare variant is
//   a one-word record; ncc's tag+union layout in miniature).
//   Tag-only enums keep bare integers — the representation is a
//   per-enum choice made at declaration, and the match walker
//   reads the flag: record scrutinees test RGET-word-0 instead of
//   the value itself. A match arm may BIND the payload — `num(x)`
//   DUPs the record, RGETs word 1, and STOREs into x before the
//   arm's chain runs; the binder is in scope for that chain only
//   and dies at the brace like every arm name.
//
//   STRUCTS (rung 1 of the struct arc — see docs/selfhost.md). A
//   declaration `struct P \{ x, y \}` is tables, not code: the name and
//   its field symbols (declarations lead the program, before fns). A
//   literal `P\{3, 4\}` allocates a flat-i64-word record in a side
//   RECORD store through a shared cursor — the string-table move — and
//   the value on the stack IS the record's base index. `p.x` is one
//   RGET at base+slot. The TYPE COLUMN grew type ids for this: nt is
//   0 int / 1 str / 2+k struct k, so the checker resolves each field
//   name against the BASE'S struct (the tree records the resolved
//   slot; emit stays type-blind, the STREQ pattern), enforces literal
//   arity and field-values-are-integers, and refuses a record index
//   everywhere a number is expected — arithmetic, print, holes, call
//   arguments — because the machine cannot tell them apart and the
//   checker can. Parse-side, `name \{` opens a literal ONLY when the
//   name is a declared struct, so `if ok \{ ... \}` keeps meaning what
//   it meant (ncc refuses cond-position literals syntactically; the
//   toy disambiguates by declaration — documented divergence). No
//   struct folding: records exist at run time only, the VM being the
//   one allocator. Rung 2 added FIELD WRITES: `p.x = e;` is a
//   statement (name '.' name '=' - all four tokens checked, so a
//   tail that merely READS a field is never taken for one); the
//   base must be a bound name holding a struct, the value an
//   integer, and the check pass resolves the field to its slot
//   exactly as reads do. And rung 3 let structs CROSS CALLS:
//   `fn bump(q: P)` DECLARES its parameter's type (': Name' after
//   a parameter, N's own syntax; unannotated = integer; the type
//   table fpt rides in Sdefs, which already reached parser and
//   checker - zero new plumbing), the call site is held to it
//   (wrong struct, int-into-struct, struct-into-plain all refused
//   with located errors), and the body sees the parameter AS its
//   struct, so field reads and writes on it just work. What
//   crosses is the record's INDEX - reference semantics, stated
//   plainly: the callee's write is visible in the caller, exactly
//   as a string crosses by its table index. Returns stay integer.
//
//   INTERPOLATION — the real lexer's hardest feature, at toy scale. A
//   double-quoted string containing a brace lexes as SEGMENTS: kind 16
//   HEAD (the text before the first hole), then the hole's expression
//   as ordinary tokens (the lexer switches to EXPRESSION mode until
//   the matching '}'), then kind 18 MID for each between-holes segment
//   or kind 17 TAIL for the text after the last — ncc's own
//   T_STR_HEAD/MID/TAIL shape, one mode flag standing in for its mode
//   stack (one level: a string literal INSIDE a hole is refused with a
//   located error, and braces in toy strings now always open a hole —
//   N's own rule, though the toy has no \{ escape). The parser then
//   DESUGARS as it parses: segments and holes join into a plain +
//   chain, each hole wrapped in an ITOS node, and the existing type
//   machinery compiles it — the checker resolves str + str to CONCAT,
//   an int hole keeps its ITOS (number to text), and a str hole's
//   ITOS REWRITES to its child in place (a string inserts as itself —
//   N's format-by-inferred-type rule, decided by the same pass that
//   decides PRINT vs PRINTS). No new machinery below the checker: the
//   sugar compiles through the sugar-free tree. And the fold pass
//   closes the story: ITOS of a constant writes its digits through
//   the shared cursor at COMPILE time, the CONCATs above collapse,
//   and a fully-constant interpolated string becomes ONE literal —
//   PUSH + PRINTS, nothing else.
//
//   THE TYPE COLUMN. Strings are real VALUES now, and the check pass
//   is what makes that safe. A double-quoted literal lexes into a
//   side STRING TABLE (sb bytes + sts/stl spans, the interned-names
//   pattern; no escapes yet — the scan runs to the next quote) and at
//   run time a string IS its table index: a strlit node lowers to a
//   plain PUSH, so strings ride the same stack, the same vars table,
//   the same STORE/LOAD as every integer. The machine cannot tell
//   them apart — which is precisely why the checker must. check_node
//   now ANNOTATES the tree: every expression node gets a type in nt
//   (0 int, 1 str), computed bottom-up; a per-symbol table (vt) gives
//   loads the type their name was bound with, and a name's type is
//   FIXED for its lifetime ("a name cannot change type" — otherwise
//   `x := 1; if c \{ x := "s"; \} print x;` would leave x's kind
//   depending on which way c went at run time). Int-only, refused
//   with located errors: arithmetic and comparisons, conditions, call
//   arguments and returns, and a body's tail value. Strings may be
//   bound, loaded, and printed. `print expr;` is ONE form — the
//   operand's inferred type picks PRINT or PRINTS at emit, which is
//   the type column doing real work: same syntax, different code,
//   decided by the checker. The VM carries the string table alongside
//   the code; PRINTS pops the index and writes the bytes through the
//   same audited boundary every other output uses.
//   And `==` over two strings is legal — and IS A DIFFERENT
//   OPERATION: the checker REWRITES the node's opcode from EQ to
//   STREQ (byte-compare the spans, push 0/1). Overload resolution at
//   toy scale: the tree records the RESOLVED operation, so the emit
//   walker stays type-blind for operators and just emits nv. Since ==
//   yields an int, `if name == "nyx" { ... }` composes with plain
//   conditions for free. Two literal strings even fold at compile
//   time (the fold pass carries the table now). `!=` resolves the
//   same way — NEQ over two strings becomes STRNEQ, the identical
//   byte-compare pushing the flipped answer, folding likewise — so
//   the equality pair is symmetric. Mixed comparisons and the
//   ordering operators on strings stay refused.
//   `+` over two strings is CONCAT — the first operator that MAKES a
//   string. The checker resolves it the same way (nv becomes opcode
//   21, and the node's type is str, so concatenations bind, print,
//   compare, and chain). At run time the VM appends a fresh table
//   entry — both spans copied to the end of the bytes buffer — and
//   pushes its index; the table's write cursors live behind a pointer
//   field (Strs.cur), seeded by the lexer and advanced by whoever
//   appends: the VM at run time, or the FOLD pass, which concatenates
//   two literals at compile time through the very same cursor — one
//   append mechanism, two moments. Runtime strings are per-program
//   transient (the next lex resets the cursors), and the toy trusts
//   its programs to fit the table, as it does every other buffer.

#[caps(syscall)]
extern syscall {
    fn write(fd: i32, buf: #[user] *u8, len: isize) -> i64 = 1
    fn sys_open(path: *u8, flags: i64, mode: i64) -> i64   = 3
    fn sys_read(fd: i64, buf: *u8, len: isize) -> i64      = 4
    fn sys_close(fd: i64) -> i64                           = 5
    fn sys_sbrk(incr: i64) -> i64                          = 7
    fn sys_exit(code: i64) -> never                        = 0
}

#[caps(syscall)]
fn put(s: str) {
    write(1, s.ptr as #[user] *u8, s.len as isize);
}

#[caps(syscall)]
fn alloc_words(n: i64) -> *i64 {
    sys_sbrk(n * 8) as *i64
}

#[caps(syscall)]
fn alloc_bytes(n: i64) -> *u8 {
    sys_sbrk(n) as *u8
}

// Write p[start .. start+len) — the audited boundary PRINTS uses to
// reach the string table's bytes (ntokens' put_span, over a *u8).
#[caps(syscall)]
fn bput(p: *u8, start: i64, len: i64) {
    write(1, (p as addr + start as addr) as #[user] *u8, len as isize);
}

fn is_digit(b: i64) -> bool { b >= 48 && b <= 57 }

// Is the span p[st .. st+len) one of N's builtin type names? The toy
// reserves them (token kind 22) so annotations and casts can name
// them - all mapping to its ONE numeric kind. i8/i16/i32/i64 and the
// u twins are shape-checked; isize/usize/addr/bool spelled out.
fn is_builtin_type(p: *u8, st: i64, len: i64) -> bool {
    b0 := p[st] as i64;
    if len == 2 && (b0 == 105 || b0 == 117) && p[st + 1] as i64 == 56 {
        return true;                       // i8 u8
    }
    if len == 3 && (b0 == 105 || b0 == 117) {
        b1 := p[st + 1] as i64;
        b2 := p[st + 2] as i64;
        if b1 == 49 && b2 == 54 { return true; }   // i16 u16
        if b1 == 51 && b2 == 50 { return true; }   // i32 u32
        if b1 == 54 && b2 == 52 { return true; }   // i64 u64
    }
    if len == 5 && (b0 == 105 || b0 == 117) && p[st + 1] as i64 == 115
        && p[st + 2] as i64 == 105 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 122
        && p[st + 4] as i64 == 101 {
        return true;                       // isize usize
    }
    if len == 4 && b0 == 97 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 100
        && p[st + 2] as i64 == 100 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 114 {
        return true;                       // addr
    }
    if len == 4 && b0 == 98 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 111
        && p[st + 2] as i64 == 111 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 108 {
        return true;                       // bool
    }
    false
}
fn is_letter(b: i64) -> bool {             // N's identifier alphabet:
    (b >= 97 && b <= 122) || (b >= 65 && b <= 90) || b == 95
}                                          // a-z A-Z _ (digits can follow
                                           // only via the number arm today)

// Scan src into the two token arrays; returns the token count. The
// last token is always the end marker (kind 9), so the parser can
// stop without carrying the count around. Identifiers intern into the
// symbol table (nb = names bytes, ss/sl = per-symbol start/len): a
// span already seen yields its existing index, a new one is appended —
// the same move ncc's vars_find/vars_add makes over its VARS table.
fn lex(p: *u8, n: i64, k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, sy: Syms, st: Strs) -> i64 {
    mut i := 0;
    mut t := 0;
    mut ns := 0;                           // interned symbol count
    mut nl := 0;                           // names buffer length
    mut sc := 0;                           // string-table entry count
    mut sbl := 0;                          // string bytes-buffer length
    mut line := 1;                         // 1-based, for diagnostics (rung 2)
    mut ls := 0;                           // index where the current line
                                           // starts — column = i - ls + 1
    mut interp := 0;                       // 1 = inside a \{...\} hole: the
                                           // lexer is in EXPRESSION mode and
                                           // '}' resumes the string
    while i < n {
        b := p[i] as i64;
        if b == 32 || b == 9 || b == 13 {  // space / tab / CR
            i += 1;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 10 {                       // newline: the diagnostic counter
            line += 1;
            i += 1;
            ls = i;                        // and the new line starts here
            continue;
        }
        if b == 47 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 47 {
            while i < n && p[i] as i64 != 10 {
                i += 1;                    // a line comment vanishes here;
            }                              // the newline arm keeps the count
            continue;
        }
        if b == 47 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 42 {
            mut depth := 1;                // '/*': a block comment, NESTING
            mut j := i + 2;                // like N's own — a depth counter,
            while depth > 0 && j < n {     // newlines still ticking the line
                c := p[j] as i64;
                if c == 10 { line += 1; j += 1; ls = j; }
                else if c == 47 && j + 1 < n && p[j + 1] as i64 == 42 {
                    depth += 1; j += 2;
                }
                else if c == 42 && j + 1 < n && p[j + 1] as i64 == 47 {
                    depth -= 1; j += 2;
                }
                else { j += 1; }
            }
            i = j;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 35 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 91 {
            mut j := i + 2;                // '#[': an attribute. The toy
            while j < n && p[j] as i64 != 93 {   // reads N's syntax and
                if p[j] as i64 == 10 {     // deliberately IGNORES the
                    line += 1;             // meaning — surface parity only:
                    ls = j + 1;            // the semantics (#[user], #[caps],
                }                          // #[drop]) belong to ncc's world,
                j += 1;                    // and pretending otherwise would
            }                              // be dishonest. Skipped like a
            i = j + 1;                     // comment, line counter ticking.
            continue;
        }
        ln[t] = line * 1000 + (i - ls + 1);   // every token records line AND
                                           // column, packed line*1000+col —
                                           // ONE array, and fail() decodes;
                                           // toy lines stay under 1000 cols
        if b == 34 {                       // string start: scan the segment
            if interp == 1 {               // (no escapes; a brace ALWAYS
                fail(line * 1000 + (i - ls + 1),
                     "no string literals inside an interpolation");
            }                              // opens a hole now, N's rule)
            mut j := i + 1;
            mut ol := 0;
            while j < n && p[j] as i64 != 34 && p[j] as i64 != 123 {
                mut c2 := p[j] as i64;
                if c2 == 92 && j + 1 < n { // a '\\' escape: N's own set;
                    e := p[j + 1] as i64;  // an unknown escape keeps both
                    mut m2 := 0;           // bytes as written
                    if e == 110 { c2 = 10; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 116 { c2 = 9; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 114 { c2 = 13; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 48 { c2 = 0; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 92 { c2 = 92; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 34 { c2 = 34; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 123 { c2 = 123; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 125 { c2 = 125; m2 = 1; }
                    if m2 == 1 { j += 1; } // an escaped brace lands as a
                }                          // byte and never reaches the
                st.sb[sbl + ol] = c2 as u8;   // hole test above
                ol += 1;
                j += 1;
            }
            st.sts[sc] = sbl;
            st.stl[sc] = ol;
            sbl += ol;
            mut kk := 10;                  // a plain literal at the quote ...
            if j < n && p[j] as i64 == 123 {
                kk = 16;                   // ... or the HEAD of an
                interp = 1;                // interpolated one at the brace:
            }                              // expression mode until '}'
            k[t] = kk; v[t] = sc; t += 1;
            sc += 1;
            i = j + 1;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 125 && interp == 1 {       // '}' closes the hole: back to
            mut j := i + 1;
            mut ol := 0;
            while j < n && p[j] as i64 != 34 && p[j] as i64 != 123 {
                mut c2 := p[j] as i64;
                if c2 == 92 && j + 1 < n { // a '\\' escape: N's own set;
                    e := p[j + 1] as i64;  // an unknown escape keeps both
                    mut m2 := 0;           // bytes as written
                    if e == 110 { c2 = 10; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 116 { c2 = 9; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 114 { c2 = 13; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 48 { c2 = 0; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 92 { c2 = 92; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 34 { c2 = 34; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 123 { c2 = 123; m2 = 1; }
                    if e == 125 { c2 = 125; m2 = 1; }
                    if m2 == 1 { j += 1; } // an escaped brace lands as a
                }                          // byte and never reaches the
                st.sb[sbl + ol] = c2 as u8;   // hole test above
                ol += 1;
                j += 1;
            }
            st.sts[sc] = sbl;
            st.stl[sc] = ol;
            sbl += ol;
            mut kk := 17;                  // the TAIL at the quote ...
            if j < n && p[j] as i64 == 123 {
                kk = 18;                   // ... or a MID: another hole opens
            }
            if kk == 17 { interp = 0; }
            k[t] = kk; v[t] = sc; t += 1;
            sc += 1;
            i = j + 1;
            continue;
        }
        if is_digit(b) {
            if b == 48 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 120 {
                mut j := i + 2;            // '0x': hexadecimal, lowercase
                mut hval := 0;             // a-f at toy scale, with '_'
                mut hgo := true;           // separators skipped like N's
                while hgo {
                    hgo = false;
                    if j < n {
                        c := p[j] as i64;
                        if is_digit(c) { hval = hval * 16 + (c - 48); j += 1; hgo = true; }
                        if c >= 97 && c <= 102 { hval = hval * 16 + (c - 87); j += 1; hgo = true; }
                        if c == 95 { j += 1; hgo = true; }
                    }
                }
                k[t] = 0; v[t] = hval; t += 1;
                i = j;
                continue;
            }
            mut val := 0;
            mut dgo := true;
            while dgo {                    // decimal, '_' separators skipped
                dgo = false;
                if i < n {
                    c := p[i] as i64;
                    if is_digit(c) { val = val * 10 + (c - 48); i += 1; dgo = true; }
                    if c == 95 { i += 1; dgo = true; }
                }
            }
            k[t] = 0; v[t] = val; t += 1;
            continue;
        }
        if is_letter(b) {                  // keyword or identifier — N's
            st := i;                       // full rule now: a letter or _
            mut j := i;                    // first, letters, digits, and _
            while j < n && (is_letter(p[j] as i64) || is_digit(p[j] as i64)) {
                j += 1;                    // after (i64 is ONE token; a
            }                              // digit-first token is a number)
            len := j - st;
            mut kind := 1;
            if len == 2 && p[st] as i64 == 105 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 102 {
                kind = 5;                  // if
            }
            if len == 2 && p[st] as i64 == 102 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 110 {
                kind = 7;                  // fn
            }
            if len == 5 && p[st] as i64 == 119 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 104
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 105 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 108
                && p[st + 4] as i64 == 101 {
                kind = 6;                  // while
            }
            if len == 4 && p[st] as i64 == 101 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 108
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 115 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 101 {
                kind = 8;                  // else
            }
            if len == 6 && p[st] as i64 == 115 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 116
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 114 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 117
                && p[st + 4] as i64 == 99 && p[st + 5] as i64 == 116 {
                kind = 19;                 // struct (a reserved word)
            }
            if len == 4 && p[st] as i64 == 101 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 110
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 117 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 109 {
                kind = 20;                 // enum
            }
            if len == 5 && p[st] as i64 == 109 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 97
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 116 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 99
                && p[st + 4] as i64 == 104 {
                kind = 21;                 // match
            }
            if len == 2 && p[st] as i64 == 97 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 115 {
                kind = 23;                 // as (the cast word)
            }
            if len == 3 && p[st] as i64 == 112 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 116
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 114 {
                kind = 24;                 // ptr (reserved at toy scale,
            }                              // like len - a str's field)
            if len == 6 && p[st] as i64 == 101 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 120
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 116 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 101
                && p[st + 4] as i64 == 114 && p[st + 5] as i64 == 110 {
                kind = 25;                 // extern
            }
            if len == 3 && p[st] as i64 == 109 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 117
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 116 {
                kind = 26;                 // mut (the mutability marker)
            }
            if kind == 1 && is_builtin_type(p, st, len) {
                kind = 22;                 // a builtin type name, reserved
            }
            if len == 5 && p[st] as i64 == 112 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 114
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 105 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 110
                && p[st + 4] as i64 == 116 {
                kind = 11;                 // print
            }
            if len == 3 && p[st] as i64 == 108 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 101
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 110 {
                kind = 15;                 // len (a reserved word now)
            }
            mut val := 0;
            if kind == 1 {                 // intern: look the span up ...
                mut idx := -1;
                mut s := 0;
                while s < ns {
                    if sy.sl[s] == len {
                        mut eq := true;
                        mut q := 0;
                        while q < len {
                            if sy.nb[sy.ss[s] + q] as i64 != p[st + q] as i64 {
                                eq = false;
                            }
                            q += 1;
                        }
                        if eq && idx < 0 { idx = s; }
                    }
                    s += 1;
                }
                if idx < 0 {               // ... or append a new symbol
                    sy.ss[ns] = nl;
                    sy.sl[ns] = len;
                    mut q := 0;
                    while q < len {
                        sy.nb[nl + q] = p[st + q];
                        q += 1;
                    }
                    nl += len;
                    idx = ns;
                    ns += 1;
                }
                val = idx;
            }
            k[t] = kind; v[t] = val; t += 1;
            i = j;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 58 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 4; v[t] = 0; t += 1;    // :=
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 61 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 3; v[t] = 0; t += 1;    // ==
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 33 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 12; v[t] = 0; t += 1;   // !=
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 60 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 13; v[t] = 0; t += 1;   // <=
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 62 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 14; v[t] = 0; t += 1;   // >=
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        k[t] = 2; v[t] = b; t += 1;        // operator / punct, by byte
        i += 1;
    }
    k[t] = 9; v[t] = 0;                    // end marker, placed like any
    ln[t] = line * 1000 + (n - ls + 1);    // token: just past the input
    st.cur[0] = sc;                        // seed the table's write cursors:
    st.cur[1] = sbl;                       // appends (fold or VM) start here
    t + 1
}

// Rung 2 of the selfhost road: the toy REFUSES bad input instead of
// wandering into undefined behavior. First error wins; the message
// carries the token's line and column. (It exits with status 0 so the test
// harness — which treats a non-zero exit as an example crash — stays
// strict for everything else; a real compiler would exit 1.)
#[caps(syscall)]
fn fail(lc: i64, msg: str) -> never {
    put("line {lc / 1000}:{lc % 1000}: error: {msg}\n");
    sys_exit(0);                           // lc = the packed line*1000+col
}                                          // every token and node carries —
                                           // one decode point, 27 callers
                                           // unchanged

// The expression tree, as parallel i64 arrays in one by-value struct:
// nk = node kind, nl/nr = child indexes, nv = the node's value (a
// number, a slot, an opcode, or a function symbol), na = the flat
// argument table (four root indexes per call node, na[idx*4 + j]),
// nline = the node's source line, so the check pass can locate blame.
struct Ast { nk: *i64, nl: *i64, nr: *i64, nv: *i64, na: *i64, nline: *i64,
             nt: *i64 }

// The string table, one by-value bundle: literal bytes + per-entry
// start/len spans + the WRITE CURSORS (cur[0] = entry count, cur[1] =
// bytes used) behind a pointer, so every by-value copy shares them —
// the lexer seeds them, and the fold pass or the VM advances them
// when a CONCAT appends. (Bundling matters practically too: N caps
// functions at 16 parameters, and the toy's plumbing was at the door.)
struct Syms { nb: *u8, ss: *i64, sl: *i64 }

// Struct definitions + the runtime record store, one bundle: sn = each
// struct's name symbol, sf = its field symbols (four slots each, the
// fp[f*4+j] move), sfc = field counts; rw = the flat record words the
// VM allocates from, and cur behind a pointer so copies share it —
// cur[0] = declared struct count, cur[1] = record words used. Both
// reset per program (parse_program seeds them, like lex seeds Strs).
// fpt = per-function declared PARAMETER types (fpt[f*4+j], the fp
// move): 0 int (the unannotated default), 2+k struct k - it lives
// here because Sdefs already reaches the parser and the checker.
// en/ev/evc mirror sn/sf/sfc for ENUMS (tag-only: a variant is its
// index), with cur[2] the declared enum count; an enum's TYPE ID is
// 100+e, far from the struct ids (2+k, k<16) on purpose. ep flags
// PAYLOAD variants (ep[e*4+v]): an enum with ANY payload variant
// makes ALL its values records in rw - tag at word 0, the payload
// word after it; a bare variant is a 1-word record. A tag-only
// enum keeps its bare-integer tags - the representation is chosen
// per enum at declaration, ncc's own tag+union layout in miniature.
// xn/xnum/xpc record EXTERN fns (name symbol, syscall number, param
// count; count in cur[3]): a call to one becomes a SYSCALL opcode
// the VM services by number - the shape is real, the kernel is the
// VM.
struct Sdefs { sn: *i64, sf: *i64, sfc: *i64, rw: *i64, cur: *i64,
               fpt: *i64, en: *i64, ev: *i64, evc: *i64, ep: *i64,
               xn: *i64, xnum: *i64, xpc: *i64 }

struct Strs { sb: *u8, sts: *i64, stl: *i64, cur: *i64 }

// The checker's environment: the bound-name list (which symbols are
// live, bv + a threaded count) and the per-symbol type table (vt —
// what kind of value each name holds, 0 int / 1 str). The flat
// runtime table cannot tell an integer from a string index; the
// checker can, and this is where it keeps the knowledge.
struct Scope { bv: *i64, vt: *i64, mv: *i64 }

// The outer skeleton's cursors: pos walks tokens, cp walks code. The
// bound-name count no longer threads through parsing — scope lives in
// the check walk, where it belongs.
struct EP { pos: i64, cp: i64 }

// The build-phase cursors: pos walks tokens, nn is the next free node,
// root is the subtree this rule just built.
struct NP { pos: i64, nn: i64, root: i64 }

fn emit1(code: *i64, cp: i64, op: i64) -> i64 {
    code[cp] = op;
    cp + 1
}

fn emit2(code: *i64, cp: i64, op: i64, arg: i64) -> i64 {
    code[cp] = op;
    code[cp + 1] = arg;
    cp + 2
}

// factor := number | name '(' cmp { ',' cmp } ')' | name
//         | '-' factor | '(' cmp ')'
//   Phase one only BUILDS: every arm appends a node and returns its
//   index — not a single opcode is emitted on this side of the seam.
//   The call arm allocates its node FIRST so the argument roots can
//   fill its slice of the arg table in place; and with the whole call
//   in hand as data, the parser finally checks it — the argument count
//   must match the callee's parameter count.
// factor := prim { '.' name }
//   Field access binds tightest, like calls — a postfix loop over the
//   primary. The node stores the field's SYMBOL; the check pass
//   resolves it to a slot offset once the base's type is known.
fn ast_factor(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    mut r := ast_prim(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos, nn);
    mut going := true;
    while going {
        going = false;
        if k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 46 && k[r.pos + 1] == 15 {
            a.nk[r.nn] = 11; a.nl[r.nn] = r.root; a.nr[r.nn] = -1;
            a.nv[r.nn] = 0;                // .len IS the len builtin - the
            a.nline[r.nn] = ln[r.pos];     // same node, opcode, and folding
            r = NP{ pos: r.pos + 2, nn: r.nn + 1, root: r.nn };
            going = true;
        }
        if k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 46 && k[r.pos + 1] == 24 {
            r = NP{ pos: r.pos + 2, nn: r.nn, root: r.root };
            going = true;                  // .ptr is the IDENTITY at toy
        }                                  // scale: a string IS its table
        if k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 46 {   // index (stated asterisk)
            if k[r.pos + 1] != 1 {
                fail(ln[r.pos + 1], "expected a field name");
            }
            a.nk[r.nn] = 15; a.nl[r.nn] = r.root; a.nr[r.nn] = -1;
            a.nv[r.nn] = v[r.pos + 1];
            a.nline[r.nn] = ln[r.pos];
            r = NP{ pos: r.pos + 2, nn: r.nn + 1, root: r.nn };
            going = true;
        }
        if k[r.pos] == 23 {                // 'as TYPE': read the cast and
            mut p3 := r.pos + 1;           // KEEP THE VALUE - every toy
            while k[p3] == 2 && v[p3] == 42 {   // value is an i64 word, so
                p3 += 1;                   // types cannot change it; the
            }                              // pointer stars and the name
            if !(k[p3] == 22 || k[p3] == 1) {   // are consumed, the node
                fail(ln[p3], "expected a type name");   // untouched (the
            }                              // stated asterisk, like
            r = NP{ pos: p3 + 1, nn: r.nn, root: r.root };   // attributes)
            going = true;
        }
    }
    r
}

fn ast_prim(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    if k[pos] == 0 {                       // number
        a.nk[nn] = 0; a.nl[nn] = 0; a.nr[nn] = 0; a.nv[nn] = v[pos];
        a.nline[nn] = ln[pos];
        return NP{ pos: pos + 1, nn: nn + 1, root: nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 10 {                      // string literal: a value now —
        a.nk[nn] = 10; a.nl[nn] = -1;      // its table index, PUSHed like
        a.nr[nn] = -1; a.nv[nn] = v[pos];  // any number; the type column
        a.nline[nn] = ln[pos];             // keeps the kinds apart
        return NP{ pos: pos + 1, nn: nn + 1, root: nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 16 {                      // interpolated string: HEAD, then
        a.nk[nn] = 10; a.nl[nn] = -1;      // hole + segment pairs until the
        a.nr[nn] = -1; a.nv[nn] = v[pos];  // TAIL. Desugared AS PARSED into
        a.nline[nn] = ln[pos];             // plain ADD nodes over the pieces
        mut cur := nn;                     // — each hole wrapped in an ITOS
        mut n2 := nn + 1;                  // node — and the CHECKER later
        mut p := pos + 1;                  // resolves str + str to CONCAT:
        mut going := true;                 // the sugar compiles through the
        while going {                      // sugar-free tree.
            r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, p, n2);
            w := r.nn;                     // the hole's expression, wrapped
            a.nk[w] = 13; a.nl[w] = r.root; a.nr[w] = -1; a.nv[w] = 0;
            a.nline[w] = ln[p];
            j1 := w + 1;                   // joined onto the chain so far
            a.nk[j1] = 2; a.nl[j1] = cur; a.nr[j1] = w; a.nv[j1] = 2;
            a.nline[j1] = ln[p];
            if !(k[r.pos] == 17 || k[r.pos] == 18) {
                fail(ln[r.pos], "unterminated interpolation");
            }
            s2 := j1 + 1;                  // the segment after the hole
            a.nk[s2] = 10; a.nl[s2] = -1; a.nr[s2] = -1; a.nv[s2] = v[r.pos];
            a.nline[s2] = ln[r.pos];
            j2 := s2 + 1;                  // joined on; a MID keeps going
            a.nk[j2] = 2; a.nl[j2] = j1; a.nr[j2] = s2; a.nv[j2] = 2;
            a.nline[j2] = ln[r.pos];
            cur = j2;
            going = k[r.pos] == 18;
            p = r.pos + 1;
            n2 = j2 + 1;
        }
        return NP{ pos: p, nn: n2, root: cur };
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 && k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 46 {
        mut eid := -1;                     // name '.' name is an ENUM TAG
        mut ei := 0;                       // when the name is a declared
        while ei < sd.cur[2] {             // enum — resolved AT PARSE, the
            if sd.en[ei] == v[pos] { eid = ei; }   // enum being known; a
            ei += 1;                       // plain variable's field read
        }                                  // is untouched (not an enum)
        if eid >= 0 {
            if k[pos + 2] != 1 {
                fail(ln[pos + 2], "expected a variant name");
            }
            mut tag := -1;
            mut vi := 0;
            while vi < sd.evc[eid] {
                if sd.ev[eid * 4 + vi] == v[pos + 2] { tag = vi; }
                vi += 1;
            }
            if tag < 0 { fail(ln[pos + 2], "unknown variant"); }
            mut haspl := 0;                // does the ENUM carry payloads?
            mut pv := 0;
            while pv < sd.evc[eid] {
                if sd.ep[eid * 4 + pv] == 1 { haspl = 1; }
                pv += 1;
            }
            if haspl == 1 {                // record representation: kind 20
                idx := nn;                 // constructs tag [+ payload]
                a.nline[idx] = ln[pos + 1];
                a.nk[idx] = 20; a.nv[idx] = tag; a.nr[idx] = eid;
                a.nl[idx] = -1;
                mut p3 := pos + 3;
                mut n3 := nn + 1;
                if sd.ep[eid * 4 + tag] == 1 {
                    if !(k[p3] == 2 && v[p3] == 40) {
                        fail(ln[p3], "the variant takes a payload");
                    }
                    r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, p3 + 1, n3);
                    if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 41) {
                        fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ')' after the payload");
                    }
                    a.nl[idx] = r.root;
                    p3 = r.pos + 1;
                    n3 = r.nn;
                }
                if sd.ep[eid * 4 + tag] == 0 && k[p3] == 2 && v[p3] == 40 {
                    fail(ln[p3], "the variant takes no payload");
                }
                return NP{ pos: p3, nn: n3, root: idx };
            }
            a.nk[nn] = 19; a.nl[nn] = -1; a.nr[nn] = eid; a.nv[nn] = tag;
            a.nline[nn] = ln[pos + 1];
            return NP{ pos: pos + 3, nn: nn + 1, root: nn };
        }
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 && k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 123 {
        mut sid := -1;                     // name '{' opens a struct literal
        mut si := 0;                       // ONLY if the name is a declared
        while si < sd.cur[0] {             // struct — `if ok { ... }` keeps
            if sd.sn[si] == v[pos] { sid = si; }   // meaning what it meant
            si += 1;                       // (ncc refuses cond-position
        }                                  // literals syntactically; the toy
        if sid >= 0 {                      // disambiguates by declaration)
            idx := nn;                     // the literal node, made first —
            a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];        // field values fill its na slice,
            mut n2 := nn + 1;              // the call-node move
            mut p2 := pos + 2;
            mut cnt := 0;
            mut more := !(k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 125);
            while more {
                more = false;
                r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, p2, n2);
                if cnt == 4 { fail(ln[p2], "wrong number of fields"); }
                a.na[idx * 4 + cnt] = r.root;
                cnt += 1;
                p2 = r.pos;
                n2 = r.nn;
                if k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 44 { p2 += 1; more = true; }
            }
            if !(k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 125) {
                fail(ln[p2], "expected '\}' after the fields");
            }
            if cnt != sd.sfc[sid] {
                fail(ln[p2], "wrong number of fields");
            }
            a.nk[idx] = 14; a.nl[idx] = cnt; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = sid;
            return NP{ pos: p2 + 1, nn: n2, root: idx };
        }
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 && k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 40 {
        mut xi := -1;                      // an EXTERN call, if the name
        mut xs := 0;                       // is a recorded binding
        while xs < sd.cur[3] {
            if sd.xn[xs] == v[pos] { xi = xs; }
            xs += 1;
        }
        if xi >= 0 {
            idx := nn;
            a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
            mut n2 := nn + 1;
            mut p2 := pos + 2;
            mut cnt := 0;
            mut more := !(k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 41);
            while more {
                more = false;
                r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, p2, n2);
                if cnt == 4 { fail(ln[p2], "wrong number of arguments"); }
                a.na[idx * 4 + cnt] = r.root;
                cnt += 1;
                p2 = r.pos;
                n2 = r.nn;
                if k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 44 { p2 += 1; more = true; }
            }
            if !(k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 41) {
                fail(ln[p2], "expected ')' after the arguments");
            }
            if cnt != sd.xpc[xi] {
                fail(ln[p2], "wrong number of arguments");
            }
            a.nk[idx] = 21; a.nl[idx] = cnt; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = xi;
            return NP{ pos: p2 + 1, nn: n2, root: idx };
        }
        f := v[pos];                       // name '(' — a call
        idx := nn;                         // the call node, made first
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
        mut n2 := nn + 1;
        mut p := pos + 2;
        mut cnt := 0;
        mut more := !(k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 41);
        while more {                       // comma-separated argument trees
            more = false;
            r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, p, n2);
            if cnt == 4 {                  // the arg table holds four
                fail(ln[p], "wrong number of arguments");
            }
            a.na[idx * 4 + cnt] = r.root;
            cnt += 1;
            p = r.pos;
            n2 = r.nn;
            if k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 44 {   // ',' — another argument
                p += 1;
                more = true;
            }
        }
        if !(k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 41) {
            fail(ln[p], "expected ')' after the arguments");
        }
        if cnt != fpc[f] {                 // the first check that reads a call
            fail(ln[p], "wrong number of arguments");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 4; a.nl[idx] = cnt; a.nr[idx] = 0; a.nv[idx] = f;
        return NP{ pos: p + 1, nn: n2, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 {                       // name read
        a.nk[nn] = 1; a.nl[nn] = 0; a.nr[nn] = 0; a.nv[nn] = v[pos];
        a.nline[nn] = ln[pos];
        return NP{ pos: pos + 1, nn: nn + 1, root: nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 2 && v[pos] == 45 {       // unary minus
        r := ast_factor(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 1, nn);
        a.nk[r.nn] = 3; a.nl[r.nn] = r.root; a.nr[r.nn] = 0; a.nv[r.nn] = 0;
        a.nline[r.nn] = ln[pos];
        return NP{ pos: r.pos, nn: r.nn + 1, root: r.nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 2 && v[pos] == 33 {       // logical not (a bare '!' — the
        r := ast_factor(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 1, nn);   // != arm ate '!='
        a.nk[r.nn] = 12; a.nl[r.nn] = r.root; a.nr[r.nn] = -1; a.nv[r.nn] = 0;
        a.nline[r.nn] = ln[pos];           // before this could ever see it)
        return NP{ pos: r.pos, nn: r.nn + 1, root: r.nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 2 && v[pos] == 40 {       // '(' — mutual recursion
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 1, nn);
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 41) {
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ')'");
        }
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: r.root };
    }
    if k[pos] == 15 {                      // len ( cmp ) — the measuring
        idx := nn;                         // builtin: str in, int out
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
        if !(k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 40) {
            fail(ln[pos], "expected '(' after len");
        }
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 2, nn + 1);
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 41) {
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ')'");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 11; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx };
    }
    fail(ln[pos], "expected an expression");
    NP{ pos: pos, nn: nn, root: 0 }        // unreachable; keeps the lowering total
}

// term := factor (('*' | '/' | '%') factor)*
//   The operator tag stored in a binop node IS its VM opcode — one
//   name for one thing, so the walker emits nv directly.
fn ast_term(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    mut r := ast_factor(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos, nn);
    mut going := true;
    while going {
        going = false;
        if k[r.pos] == 2 && (v[r.pos] == 42 || v[r.pos] == 47 || v[r.pos] == 37) {
            op := v[r.pos];
            opl := ln[r.pos];
            n := ast_factor(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, r.pos + 1, r.nn);
            mut oc := 4;                   // MUL
            if op == 47 { oc = 5; }        // DIV
            if op == 37 { oc = 6; }        // MOD
            a.nk[n.nn] = 2; a.nl[n.nn] = r.root; a.nr[n.nn] = n.root; a.nv[n.nn] = oc;
            a.nline[n.nn] = opl;
            r = NP{ pos: n.pos, nn: n.nn + 1, root: n.nn };
            going = true;
        }
    }
    r
}

// sum := term (('+' | '-') term)*
fn ast_sum(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    mut r := ast_term(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos, nn);
    mut going := true;
    while going {
        going = false;
        if k[r.pos] == 2 && (v[r.pos] == 43 || v[r.pos] == 45) {
            op := v[r.pos];
            opl := ln[r.pos];
            n := ast_term(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, r.pos + 1, r.nn);
            mut oc := 2;                   // ADD
            if op == 45 { oc = 3; }        // SUB
            a.nk[n.nn] = 2; a.nl[n.nn] = r.root; a.nr[n.nn] = n.root; a.nv[n.nn] = oc;
            a.nline[n.nn] = opl;
            r = NP{ pos: n.pos, nn: n.nn + 1, root: n.nn };
            going = true;
        }
    }
    r
}

// cmp := sum [('<' | '>' | '==' | '!=' | '<=' | '>=') sum]
//   — lowest tier, pushes 0 or 1: the full comparison set.
fn ast_cmp(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    l := ast_sum(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos, nn);
    two := k[l.pos] == 3 || k[l.pos] == 12 || k[l.pos] == 13 || k[l.pos] == 14;
    one := k[l.pos] == 2 && (v[l.pos] == 60 || v[l.pos] == 62);
    if two || one {
        mut oc := 10;                      // EQ
        if one && v[l.pos] == 60 { oc = 8; }    // LT
        if one && v[l.pos] == 62 { oc = 9; }    // GT
        if k[l.pos] == 12 { oc = 22; }          // NEQ
        if k[l.pos] == 13 { oc = 23; }          // LE
        if k[l.pos] == 14 { oc = 24; }          // GE
        opl := ln[l.pos];
        r := ast_sum(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, l.pos + 1, l.nn);
        a.nk[r.nn] = 2; a.nl[r.nn] = l.root; a.nr[r.nn] = r.root; a.nv[r.nn] = oc;
        a.nline[r.nn] = opl;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos, nn: r.nn + 1, root: r.nn };
    }
    l
}

// THE CHECK PASS — the first consumer of the tree that is not the
// emitter, and the reason the tree exists: it looks at a whole
// expression while NO code for it has been generated yet, and emits
// nothing itself. Two duties now. SCOPE: every load must name a BOUND
// variable — a parameter of the enclosing function, or a name a `:=`
// statement already bound in this scope (env.bv holds their symbol
// indexes, bc many of them are live). TYPES: the walk ANNOTATES the
// tree bottom-up — every node's kind of value lands in nt (0 int,
// 1 str): numbers and calls are int, literals are str, a load carries
// the type its name was bound with (env.vt), and the int-only
// operations refuse strings with located errors. The predicted shape
// held: rules accrete on this one walk — which is exactly how ncc's
// check_expr grew.
fn check_node(a: Ast, sd: Sdefs, env: Scope, bc: i64, idx: i64) {
    if a.nk[idx] == 0 {                    // number: int, by construction
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 10 {                   // string literal: str
        a.nt[idx] = 1;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 19 {                   // enum tag: typed by its enum
        a.nt[idx] = 100 + a.nr[idx];
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 21 {                   // extern call: arguments are
        mut q := 0;                        // integers or strings (a string
        while q < a.nl[idx] {              // crosses as its table index -
            check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
            if a.nt[a.na[idx * 4 + q]] >= 2 {
                fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot pass a struct to a syscall");
            }
            q += 1;                        // .ptr made that literal); the
        }                                  // result is an integer
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 20 {                   // variant construction: the
        if a.nl[idx] > -1 {                // payload (if any) is an int,
            check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);   // the value is its
            if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] != 0 {      // enum
                fail(a.nline[idx], "a payload holds an integer");
            }
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 100 + a.nr[idx];
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 1 {                    // load: bound, and typed as bound
        mut ok := false;
        mut i := 0;
        while i < bc {
            if env.bv[i] == a.nv[idx] { ok = true; }
            i += 1;
        }
        if !ok {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "unknown variable");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = env.vt[a.nv[idx]];
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 2 {                    // binop: children first, then the
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]); // types resolve the OPERATION
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nr[idx]);
        lt := a.nt[a.nl[idx]];
        rt := a.nt[a.nr[idx]];
        mut resolved := false;
        mut ty := 0;
        if a.nv[idx] == 10 && lt == 1 && rt == 1 {
            a.nv[idx] = 20;                // == over two strings IS STREQ:
            resolved = true;               // the checker rewrites the opcode,
        }                                  // emit stays type-blind
        if a.nv[idx] == 22 && lt == 1 && rt == 1 {
            a.nv[idx] = 27;                // != over two strings IS STRNEQ —
            resolved = true;               // the same rewrite, the answer
        }                                  // flipped; equality symmetric now
        if a.nv[idx] == 2 && lt == 1 && rt == 1 {
            a.nv[idx] = 21;                // + over two strings IS CONCAT —
            resolved = true;               // the first operator that MAKES a
            ty = 1;                        // string, so the result is str
        }
        if !resolved && (lt == 1 || rt == 1) {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot use a string in arithmetic or comparison");
        }
        if !resolved && (lt >= 100 || rt >= 100) {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot use an enum tag in arithmetic or comparison");
        }
        if !resolved && (lt >= 2 || rt >= 2) {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot use a struct in arithmetic or comparison");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = ty;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 14 {                   // struct literal: every field
        mut q := 0;                        // value an integer at this rung,
        while q < a.nl[idx] {              // and the value's type IS the
            check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
            if a.nt[a.na[idx * 4 + q]] != 0 {
                fail(a.nline[idx], "a field holds an integer");
            }
            q += 1;
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 2 + a.nv[idx];         // struct: type id 2+sid
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 15 {                   // field read: the base must be a
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);   // struct and the name one of
        bt := a.nt[a.nl[idx]];             // ITS fields — then the tree
        if bt == 1 {                       // records the RESOLVED slot and
            fail(a.nline[idx], "a string has ptr and len only");
        }                                  // emit stays type-blind
        if bt < 2 || bt >= 100 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "only a struct value has fields");
        }
        sid := bt - 2;
        mut off := -1;
        mut q := 0;
        while q < sd.sfc[sid] {
            if sd.sf[sid * 4 + q] == a.nv[idx] { off = q; }
            q += 1;
        }
        if off < 0 { fail(a.nline[idx], "unknown field"); }
        a.nv[idx] = off;
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 3 {                    // unary minus: an int operand
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] == 1 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot negate a string");
        }
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] >= 2 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot negate a struct");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 11 {                   // len: measures a STRING, is int
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] != 1 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "len takes a string");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 12 {                   // logical not: int in, int out
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] != 0 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "! takes an integer");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 13 {                   // interpolation hole: an integer
        c := a.nl[idx];                    // becomes text (ITOS stays); a
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, c);         // string inserts as ITSELF — the
        if a.nt[c] >= 100 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot interpolate an enum tag");
        }
        if a.nt[c] >= 2 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot interpolate a struct");
        }
        if a.nt[c] == 1 {                  // node REWRITES to its child (the
            a.nk[idx] = a.nk[c];           // checker's in-place move again),
            a.nl[idx] = a.nl[c];           // and the surrounding ADD chain
            a.nr[idx] = a.nr[c];           // then resolves to CONCAT on its
            a.nv[idx] = a.nv[c];           // own — N's by-inferred-type
            a.na[idx * 4] = a.na[c * 4];   // interpolation rule, at toy
            a.na[idx * 4 + 1] = a.na[c * 4 + 1];   // scale
            a.na[idx * 4 + 2] = a.na[c * 4 + 2];
            a.na[idx * 4 + 3] = a.na[c * 4 + 3];
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 1;                     // either way the hole yields str
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 4 {                    // call: int arguments, int result
        mut q := 0;
        while q < a.nl[idx] {
            check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
            at := a.nt[a.na[idx * 4 + q]];
            want := sd.fpt[a.nv[idx] * 4 + q];
            if at == 1 && want != 1 {
                fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot pass a string to a function");
            }
            if at >= 100 && want == 0 {    // a tag or a struct into a
                fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot pass an enum tag to a plain parameter");
            }
            if at >= 2 && want == 0 {      // plain parameter: refused
                fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot pass a struct to a function");
            }
            if at != want {                // int into a struct parameter,
                fail(a.nline[idx], "argument type does not match the parameter");
            }                              // or the WRONG struct
            q += 1;
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
}

// THE FIRST TRANSFORM: constant folding, in place. Children first, so
// nested constant expressions collapse bottom-up ((2*3)+4 folds the
// MUL, sees two numbers at the ADD, folds again). A rewritten node
// simply changes kind to 0 with the computed value — its old children
// stay allocated but unreachable, which a transient per-expression
// tree can afford. DIV/MOD by a literal zero stay unfolded (zerodiv):
// the compiler must not crash computing what the program would.
fn fold_node(a: Ast, st: Strs, idx: i64) {
    if a.nk[idx] == 2 {                    // binop: fold children, then try
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        fold_node(a, st, a.nr[idx]);
        l := a.nl[idx];
        r := a.nr[idx];
        if a.nk[l] == 0 && a.nk[r] == 0 {
            x := a.nv[l];
            y := a.nv[r];
            oc := a.nv[idx];
            zerodiv := (oc == 5 || oc == 6) && y == 0;
            if !zerodiv {
                mut val := 0;
                if oc == 2 { val = x + y; }
                if oc == 3 { val = x - y; }
                if oc == 4 { val = x * y; }
                if oc == 5 { val = x / y; }
                if oc == 6 { val = x % y; }
                if oc == 8 { if x < y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 9 { if x > y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 10 { if x == y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 22 { if x != y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 23 { if x <= y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 24 { if x >= y { val = 1; } }
                a.nk[idx] = 0;             // the operation happened NOW
                a.nv[idx] = val;
            }
        }
        if (a.nv[idx] == 20 || a.nv[idx] == 27) && a.nk[l] == 10 && a.nk[r] == 10 {
            mut eq := 0;                   // two literals: compare them NOW
            if st.stl[a.nv[l]] == st.stl[a.nv[r]] {
                eq = 1;
                mut q := 0;
                while q < st.stl[a.nv[l]] {
                    if st.sb[st.sts[a.nv[l]] + q] as i64 != st.sb[st.sts[a.nv[r]] + q] as i64 {
                        eq = 0;
                    }
                    q += 1;
                }
            }
            if a.nv[idx] == 27 {           // != is the same compare with
                eq = 1 - eq;               // the answer flipped
            }
            a.nk[idx] = 0;                 // the comparison happened at
            a.nv[idx] = eq;                // compile time
        }
        if a.nv[idx] == 21 && a.nk[l] == 10 && a.nk[r] == 10 {
            base := st.cur[1];             // two literals: append the joined
            mut q := 0;                    // entry NOW, through the same
            while q < st.stl[a.nv[l]] {    // cursor the VM would use
                st.sb[base + q] = st.sb[st.sts[a.nv[l]] + q];
                q += 1;
            }
            mut w := 0;
            while w < st.stl[a.nv[r]] {
                st.sb[base + st.stl[a.nv[l]] + w] = st.sb[st.sts[a.nv[r]] + w];
                w += 1;
            }
            ni := st.cur[0];
            st.sts[ni] = base;
            st.stl[ni] = st.stl[a.nv[l]] + st.stl[a.nv[r]];
            st.cur[0] = ni + 1;
            st.cur[1] = base + st.stl[ni];
            a.nk[idx] = 10;                // the node becomes that literal
            a.nv[idx] = ni;
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 3 {                    // negation of a folded number
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        c := a.nl[idx];
        if a.nk[c] == 0 {
            a.nk[idx] = 0;
            a.nv[idx] = -a.nv[c];
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 11 {                   // len of a literal: its span
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);       // length is known right now
        c := a.nl[idx];
        if a.nk[c] == 10 {
            a.nk[idx] = 0;
            a.nv[idx] = st.stl[a.nv[c]];
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 12 {                   // not of a folded number
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        c := a.nl[idx];
        if a.nk[c] == 0 {
            mut nv := 0;
            if a.nv[c] == 0 { nv = 1; }
            a.nk[idx] = 0;
            a.nv[idx] = nv;
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 13 {                   // itos of a folded number: the
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);       // digits are known right now —
        c := a.nl[idx];                    // append the entry at compile
        if a.nk[c] == 0 {                  // time through the shared cursor
            mut x := a.nv[c];
            base := st.cur[1];
            mut len := 0;
            if x < 0 {
                st.sb[base] = 45 as u8;
                len = 1;
                x = -x;
            }
            mut m := 1;                    // digits forward by place value
            while x / m >= 10 { m = m * 10; }
            while m > 0 {
                st.sb[base + len] = (48 + (x / m) % 10) as u8;
                len += 1;
                m = m / 10;
            }
            ni := st.cur[0];
            st.sts[ni] = base;
            st.stl[ni] = len;
            st.cur[0] = ni + 1;
            st.cur[1] = base + len;
            a.nk[idx] = 10;                // the node becomes that literal —
            a.nv[idx] = ni;                // and the CONCAT folds above can
        }                                  // then collapse the whole string
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 4 {                    // call: arguments fold, calls never
        mut q := 0;
        while q < a.nl[idx] {
            fold_node(a, st, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
            q += 1;
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 14 {                   // struct literal: field VALUES
        mut q := 0;                        // fold, the literal itself never
        while q < a.nl[idx] {              // — records exist at run time
            fold_node(a, st, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);   // only, by design: the VM
            q += 1;                        // is the one allocator (fold has
        }                                  // no record table on purpose)
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 15 {                   // field read: the base folds
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);       // (its constants do); the read
    }                                      // itself is runtime
    if a.nk[idx] == 20 {                   // variant construction: the
        if a.nl[idx] > -1 {                // payload folds, the record is
            fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);   // runtime-only like every record
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 21 {                   // extern call: arguments fold,
        mut q := 0;                        // the crossing never does
        while q < a.nl[idx] {
            fold_node(a, st, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
            q += 1;
        }
    }
}

// Phase two: the postorder walk. Children first, the node's own
// operation last — and the walker emits byte-for-byte what the fused
// parser-emitter used to, no matter how the tree was built. The
// caller-save discipline lives HERE now: parse decided WHAT (a call of
// f with these argument trees), this decides HOW — save the callee's
// slots in order, evaluate all the argument trees, bind in reverse
// (pops hand back the last argument first), CALL, then restore each
// saved slot in reverse with SWAP + STORE beneath the returned value.
fn emit_node(a: Ast, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, code: *i64, cp: i64, idx: i64) -> i64 {
    if a.nk[idx] == 0 {                    // number: PUSH its value
        return emit2(code, cp, 1, a.nv[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 1 {                    // name read: LOAD its slot
        return emit2(code, cp, 11, a.nv[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 10 {                   // string literal: at run time a
        return emit2(code, cp, 1, a.nv[idx]);   // string IS its table index
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 19 {                   // enum tag: at run time a tag IS
        return emit2(code, cp, 1, a.nv[idx]);   // its index, PUSHed plain
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 21 {                   // extern call: arguments in
        mut c := cp;                       // order, then one SYSCALL with
        mut q := 0;                        // the binding index - the VM
        while q < a.nl[idx] {              // services it by number
            c = emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
            q += 1;
        }
        return emit2(code, c, 34, a.nv[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 20 {                   // variant construction: the tag
        c := emit2(code, cp, 1, a.nv[idx]);   // at word 0, the payload (if
        if a.nl[idx] > -1 {                // any) after it - RNEW builds
            c2 := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.nl[idx]);
            return emit2(code, c2, 29, 2); // the record, reused as-is
        }
        return emit2(code, c, 29, 1);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 2 {                    // binop: lhs, rhs, then the opcode
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        c2 := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.nr[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c2, a.nv[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 3 {                    // unary minus: operand, then NEG
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c, 7);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 11 {                   // len: operand, then STRLEN
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c, 25);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 12 {                   // logical not: operand, then NOT
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c, 26);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 13 {                   // itos: operand, then ITOS
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c, 28);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 14 {                   // struct literal: field values in
        mut c := cp;                       // order, then RNEW with the count
        mut q := 0;
        while q < a.nl[idx] {
            c = emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
            q += 1;
        }
        return emit2(code, c, 29, a.nl[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 15 {                   // field read: base, then RGET
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);   // with the slot the
        return emit2(code, c, 30, a.nv[idx]);   // check pass resolved
    }
    f := a.nv[idx];                        // call
    cnt := a.nl[idx];
    mut c := cp;
    mut j := 0;
    while j < cnt {                        // save the callee's slots, in order
        c = emit2(code, c, 11, fp[f * 4 + j]);
        j += 1;
    }
    mut q := 0;
    while q < cnt {                        // all argument trees, before any binds
        c = emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
        q += 1;
    }
    mut w := cnt - 1;
    while w > -1 {                         // bind in reverse: pops give the
        c = emit2(code, c, 12, fp[f * 4 + w]);   // last argument first
        w -= 1;
    }
    c = emit2(code, c, 15, fe[f]);         // CALL the entry
    mut u := cnt - 1;
    while u > -1 {                         // restore in reverse, beneath the
        c = emit1(code, c, 17);            // returned value: SWAP + STORE
        c = emit2(code, c, 12, fp[f * 4 + u]);
        u -= 1;
    }
    c
}

// A statement begins with `if`, `while`, `print`, a name + ':=', a
// FIELD WRITE — name '.' name '=' exactly, all four tokens checked, so
// a tail expression that merely READS a field (`p.x`) is never taken
// for one — or a CALL STATEMENT: name '(' ... ')' ';'. That last one
// takes real lookahead, the toy's first: the scan walks to the
// matching ')' (nesting counted) and asks for the ';' that makes the
// call a statement — a call without one is the tail. N's own rule,
// token for token: statements end in ';', the tail does not.
fn is_stmt_start(k: *i64, v: *i64, pos: i64) -> bool {
    if k[pos] == 5 || k[pos] == 6 || k[pos] == 11 || k[pos] == 21
        || k[pos] == 26 {
        return true;
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 && k[pos + 1] == 4 {
        return true;
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 && k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 46
        && k[pos + 2] == 1 && k[pos + 3] == 2 && v[pos + 3] == 61 {
        return true;
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 && k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 61 {
        return true;
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 && k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 40 {
        mut p2 := pos + 2;
        mut depth := 1;
        while depth > 0 {
            if k[p2] == 9 { return false; }
            if k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 40 { depth += 1; }
            if k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 41 { depth -= 1; }
            p2 += 1;
        }
        return k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 59;
    }
    false
}

// stmt := name ':=' cmp ';' | 'if' cmp block [ 'else' block ]
//       | 'while' cmp block | name '(' args ')' ';'
//   Statements BUILD nodes now, like every expression rule above: the
//   statement node is allocated first (the call-node trick), its
//   pieces land in nl/nr/na, and not one opcode is emitted here. The
//   jump-patching craft lives in emit_stmt.
fn ast_stmt(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    if k[pos] == 5 {                       // if cmp block [else block]
        idx := nn;
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
        c := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 1, nn + 1);
        t := ast_block(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, c.pos, c.nn);
        a.nk[idx] = 6; a.nl[idx] = c.root; a.nr[idx] = t.root; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        a.na[idx * 4] = -1;                // no else (yet)
        if k[t.pos] == 8 {
            if k[t.pos + 1] == 5 {         // else IF: the chain. The else
                e := ast_stmt(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, t.pos + 1, t.nn);
                a.na[idx * 4] = e.root;    // arm IS the next if statement —
                return NP{ pos: e.pos, nn: e.nn, root: idx };
            }                              // no walker cares: the else slot
            e := ast_block(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, t.pos + 1, t.nn);
            a.na[idx * 4] = e.root;        // held any statement all along
            return NP{ pos: e.pos, nn: e.nn, root: idx };
        }
        return NP{ pos: t.pos, nn: t.nn, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos] == 6 {                       // while cmp block
        idx := nn;
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
        c := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 1, nn + 1);
        b := ast_block(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, c.pos, c.nn);
        a.nk[idx] = 7; a.nl[idx] = c.root; a.nr[idx] = b.root; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        return NP{ pos: b.pos, nn: b.nn, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos] == 11 {                      // print cmp ; — ONE form: the
        idx := nn;                         // operand's TYPE picks the
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];            // opcode at emit
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 1, nn + 1);
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 59) {
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ';' after print");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 9; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos] == 21 {                      // match cmp { arm... } — the
        idx := nn;                         // arms are a cons list of kind-18
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];            // nodes (nv = variant symbol, nl
        c := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 1, nn + 1);   // = chain)
        if !(k[c.pos] == 2 && v[c.pos] == 123) {
            fail(ln[c.pos], "expected '\{' after the match value");
        }
        mut p2 := c.pos + 1;
        mut n2 := c.nn;
        mut first := -1;
        mut prev := -1;
        mut more := k[p2] == 1;
        while more {
            more = false;
            arm := n2;                     // variant name, an optional
            a.nk[arm] = 18; a.nv[arm] = v[p2]; a.nr[arm] = -1;
            a.nline[arm] = ln[p2];
            a.na[arm * 4] = -1;            // '(binder)', then its block
            mut p2b := p2 + 1;
            if k[p2b] == 2 && v[p2b] == 40 {
                if k[p2b + 1] != 1 {
                    fail(ln[p2b + 1], "expected a binder name");
                }
                if !(k[p2b + 2] == 2 && v[p2b + 2] == 41) {
                    fail(ln[p2b + 2], "expected ')' after the binder");
                }
                a.na[arm * 4] = v[p2b + 1];
                p2b += 3;
            }
            b := ast_block(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, p2b, n2 + 1);
            a.nl[arm] = b.root;
            if prev > -1 { a.nr[prev] = arm; } else { first = arm; }
            prev = arm;
            p2 = b.pos;
            n2 = b.nn;
            if k[p2] == 1 { more = true; }
        }
        if !(k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 125) {
            fail(ln[p2], "expected '\}' after the match arms");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 17; a.nl[idx] = c.root; a.nr[idx] = first; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        return NP{ pos: p2 + 1, nn: n2, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos] == 26 {                      // 'mut' name ':=' cmp ';' — a
        if k[pos + 1] != 1 {               // MUTABLE binding: same node as
            fail(ln[pos + 1], "expected a name after mut");
        }                                  // a plain bind, na[0] carries
        if k[pos + 2] != 4 {               // the mut flag for the checker
            fail(ln[pos + 2], "expected ':=' after the name");
        }
        idx := nn;
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos + 1];
        slot := v[pos + 1];
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 3, nn + 1);
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 59) {
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ';' after the binding");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 5; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = slot;
        a.na[idx * 4] = 1;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 61 {
        idx := nn;                         // name '=' cmp ';' — ASSIGNMENT:
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];            // a STORE to a name that must
        a.nv[idx] = v[pos];                // be bound mut — the checker
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 2, nn + 1);   // holds it
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 59) {
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ';' after the assignment");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 23; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 40 {
        idx := nn;                         // name '(' args ')' ';' — a CALL
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];            // STATEMENT: the value is made,
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos, nn + 1);   // then dropped
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 59) {
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ';' after the call");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 22; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 46 {
        idx := nn;                         // name '.' name '=' cmp ';' — a
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos + 1];        // FIELD WRITE. The base is a
        a.na[idx * 4] = v[pos];            // bound NAME (its symbol in the
        a.nv[idx] = v[pos + 2];            // arg slice), the field symbol
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 4, nn + 1);   // sits in nv
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 59) {   // until check resolves it
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ';' after the field write");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 16; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx };
    }
    idx := nn;                             // name ':=' cmp ';'
    a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
    slot := v[pos];
    r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, pos + 2, nn + 1);
    if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 59) {
        fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ';' after the binding");
    }
    a.nk[idx] = 5; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = slot;
    a.na[idx * 4] = 0;                     // bound immutable — N's default
    NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx }
}

// block := '{' { stmt } '}'   — a seq CHAIN in the node arrays: each
//   link is a seq node (nl = the statement, nr = the next link, -1
//   ends it), patched forward as statements arrive. An empty block is
//   root -1. Scope needs no bookkeeping here any more — the check walk
//   owns it.
fn ast_block(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    if !(k[pos] == 2 && v[pos] == 123) {
        fail(ln[pos], "expected '\{'");
    }
    mut p := pos + 1;                      // skip '{'
    mut n2 := nn;
    mut head := -1;
    mut prev := -1;
    mut more := true;
    while more {
        more = false;
        if is_stmt_start(k, v, p) {
            s := ast_stmt(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, p, n2);
            seq := s.nn;                   // the chain link
            a.nk[seq] = 8; a.nl[seq] = s.root; a.nr[seq] = -1; a.nv[seq] = 0;
            a.nline[seq] = a.nline[s.root];
            if prev > -1 { a.nr[prev] = seq; } else { head = seq; }
            prev = seq;
            n2 = s.nn + 1;
            p = s.pos;
            more = true;
        }
    }
    if !(k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 125) {
        fail(ln[p], "expected '\}' or a statement");
    }
    NP{ pos: p + 1, nn: n2, root: head }   // skip '}'
}

// The statement half of the CHECK pass: a seq chain threads the bound
// count forward and returns the new count; a bind adds its name only
// AFTER its right-hand side checks (`x := x + 1` cannot self-feed);
// if arms and while bodies check with the count they were given and
// this fn hands back the ORIGINAL — branch names die with the branch.
fn check_stmt(a: Ast, sd: Sdefs, env: Scope, bc: i64, idx: i64) -> i64 {
    if idx < 0 {                           // an empty chain binds nothing
        return bc;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 8 {                    // seq: this statement, then the rest
        b := check_stmt(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        return check_stmt(a, sd, env, b, a.nr[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 5 {                    // bind: rhs first, THEN the name.
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        slot := a.nv[idx];                 // ':=' always DECLARES — and the
        mut seen := false;                 // toy's flat symbol-keyed table
        mut i := 0;                        // cannot shadow (ncc can: inner-
        while i < bc {                     // most wins), so a re-':=' of a
            if env.bv[i] == slot { seen = true; }   // LIVE name is refused
            i += 1;                        // instead of silently mutating —
        }                                  // the stated divergence
        if seen {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "already bound: assign to a mut name (the toy cannot shadow)");
        }
        env.vt[slot] = a.nt[a.nl[idx]];
        env.mv[slot] = a.na[idx * 4];      // 1 = bound with mut
        env.bv[bc] = slot;
        return bc + 1;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 23 {                   // assignment: the name must be
        slot := a.nv[idx];                 // BOUND, MUT, and keep its type
        mut seen := false;                 // — ncc's v0.2 rule, at toy
        mut i := 0;                        // scale
        while i < bc {
            if env.bv[i] == slot { seen = true; }
            i += 1;
        }
        if !seen {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "unknown variable");
        }
        if env.mv[slot] != 1 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot assign to an immutable name (declare it with mut)");
        }
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if env.vt[slot] != a.nt[a.nl[idx]] {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "a name cannot change type");
        }
        return bc;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 17 {                   // match: the value must be an
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);   // enum tag, every arm must
        mt := a.nt[a.nl[idx]];             // name one of ITS variants, no
        if mt < 100 {                      // arm twice, and EVERY variant
            fail(a.nline[idx], "match takes an enum tag");
        }                                  // must be covered — the checker
        eid := mt - 100;                   // owns exhaustiveness
        mut haspl := 0;                    // record-represented enum? the
        mut pv := 0;                       // emit walker needs to know -
        while pv < sd.evc[eid] {           // the node's nv carries the flag
            if sd.ep[eid * 4 + pv] == 1 { haspl = 1; }
            pv += 1;
        }
        a.nv[idx] = haspl;
        mut covered := 0;
        mut arm := a.nr[idx];
        while arm > -1 {
            mut tag := -1;
            mut vi := 0;
            while vi < sd.evc[eid] {
                if sd.ev[eid * 4 + vi] == a.nv[arm] { tag = vi; }
                vi += 1;
            }
            if tag < 0 { fail(a.nline[arm], "unknown variant"); }
            mut bit := 1;
            mut q := 0;
            while q < tag { bit = bit * 2; q += 1; }
            if covered / bit % 2 == 1 {
                fail(a.nline[arm], "duplicate match arm");
            }
            covered += bit;
            a.nv[arm] = tag;               // the arm records its TAG now
            mut abc := bc;
            if a.na[arm * 4] > -1 {        // a binder needs a payload
                if sd.ep[eid * 4 + tag] == 0 {
                    fail(a.nline[arm], "the variant has no payload to bind");
                }
                env.bv[abc] = a.na[arm * 4];   // born bound, an integer,
                env.vt[a.na[arm * 4]] = 0; // for this arm's chain only -
                env.mv[a.na[arm * 4]] = 0; // and immutable, like a param
                abc += 1;                  // arm names die at the brace
            }
            check_stmt(a, sd, env, abc, a.nl[arm]);
            arm = a.nr[arm];
        }
        mut want := 1;
        mut w := 0;
        while w < sd.evc[eid] { want = want * 2; w += 1; }
        if covered != want - 1 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "match must cover every variant");
        }
        return bc;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 16 {                   // field write: the base must be a
        base := a.na[idx * 4];             // BOUND name holding a struct,
        mut ok := false;                   // the field one of ITS fields,
        mut i := 0;                        // the value an integer — and the
        while i < bc {                     // tree keeps the resolved slot
            if env.bv[i] == base { ok = true; }
            i += 1;
        }
        if !ok {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "unknown variable");
        }
        bt := env.vt[base];
        if bt < 2 || bt >= 100 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "only a struct value has fields");
        }
        sid := bt - 2;
        mut off := -1;
        mut q := 0;
        while q < sd.sfc[sid] {
            if sd.sf[sid * 4 + q] == a.nv[idx] { off = q; }
            q += 1;
        }
        if off < 0 { fail(a.nline[idx], "unknown field"); }
        a.nv[idx] = off;
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] != 0 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "a field holds an integer");
        }
        return bc;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 22 {                   // call statement: the value is
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);   // checked like any — and
        return bc;                         // dropped; nothing binds
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 6 {                    // if: an int condition; both arms
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] != 0 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "a condition must be an integer");
        }
        check_stmt(a, sd, env, bc, a.nr[idx]);
        check_stmt(a, sd, env, bc, a.na[idx * 4]);
        return bc;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 9 {                    // print: an int or a str — the
        check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]); // operand's nt picks the
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] >= 100 {        // opcode at emit; tags and
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot print an enum tag - match on it");
        }
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] >= 2 {          // structs have no printed form
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot print a struct - read a field");
        }
        return bc;
    }
    check_node(a, sd, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);     // while: int cond + body, restored
    if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] != 0 {
        fail(a.nline[idx], "a condition must be an integer");
    }
    check_stmt(a, sd, env, bc, a.nr[idx]);
    bc
}

// The statement half of the FOLD pass: recurse to every expression
// root and fold it — and rewrite STATEMENTS whose shape the folded
// constants decide. An if over a constant condition dissolves into
// its live arm: the node is rewritten in place into a seq wrapper
// around that arm's chain (a seq in statement position nests fine —
// every walker handles kind 8 and -1 generically, so no other pass
// changed at all). The dead arm is left where it stands, unreachable
// from the tree — never emitted. A while over constant FALSE becomes
// an empty wrapper the same way; over constant TRUE it is left alone
// (an infinite loop is the program's right), body still folding.
fn fold_stmt(a: Ast, st: Strs, idx: i64) {
    if idx < 0 {
        return;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 8 { fold_stmt(a, st, a.nl[idx]); fold_stmt(a, st, a.nr[idx]); }
    if a.nk[idx] == 5 { fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]); }
    if a.nk[idx] == 9 { fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]); }
    if a.nk[idx] == 16 { fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]); }   // the written value folds
    if a.nk[idx] == 22 { fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]); }   // the dropped value folds
    if a.nk[idx] == 23 { fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]); }   // the assigned value folds
    if a.nk[idx] == 17 {                   // match: the value and every arm
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);       // fold; the statement itself does
        mut arm := a.nr[idx];              // not (a constant scrutinee is a
        while arm > -1 {                   // later rung, like dead arms was)
            fold_stmt(a, st, a.nl[arm]);
            arm = a.nr[arm];
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 6 {
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nk[a.nl[idx]] == 0 {          // constant condition: dead-arm
            mut live := a.nr[idx];         // elimination — nonzero keeps
            if a.nv[a.nl[idx]] == 0 {      // then, zero keeps else
                live = a.na[idx * 4];
            }
            fold_stmt(a, st, live);
            a.nk[idx] = 8;                 // the if dissolves into a seq
            a.nl[idx] = live;              // wrapper around the live arm
            a.nr[idx] = -1;                // (or around nothing at all)
            return;
        }
        fold_stmt(a, st, a.nr[idx]);
        fold_stmt(a, st, a.na[idx * 4]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 7 {
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nk[a.nl[idx]] == 0 && a.nv[a.nl[idx]] == 0 {
            a.nk[idx] = 8;                 // while false: it never runs —
            a.nl[idx] = -1;                // an empty wrapper remains
            a.nr[idx] = -1;
            return;
        }
        fold_stmt(a, st, a.nr[idx]);
    }
}

// The statement half of the EMIT walk — the jump-patching craft moved
// here from the old emitting parser, unchanged in shape: the target is
// not known until the arm has been emitted, so JZ takes a placeholder
// and a later index store patches it; `while` emits its back-JMP and
// the JZ patch lands past it; `else` is the classic diamond.
fn emit_stmt(a: Ast, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, code: *i64, cp: i64, idx: i64) -> i64 {
    if idx < 0 {                           // an empty chain emits nothing
        return cp;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 8 {                    // seq: statement, then the rest
        c := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.nr[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 5 {                    // bind: rhs, then STORE its slot
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit2(code, c, 12, a.nv[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 9 {                    // print: the operand's TYPE picks
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] == 1 {          // the opcode — the type column
            return emit1(code, c, 19);     // doing real work
        }
        return emit1(code, c, 18);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 17 {                   // match: the tag on the stack,
        mut c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        mut ends0 := 0;                    // then per arm DUP/PUSH/EQ/JZ —
        mut ends1 := 0;                    // the last arm needs NO test,
        mut ends2 := 0;                    // exhaustiveness proved it (up
        mut ne := 0;                       // to three earlier arms hold
        mut arm := a.nr[idx];              // JMP-to-end holes)
        haspl := a.nv[idx];                // 1 = record scrutinee: the
        while arm > -1 {                   // tag lives at word 0 and a
            if a.nr[arm] > -1 {            // binder takes word 1
                c = emit1(code, c, 32);    // DUP the scrutinee
                if haspl == 1 {
                    c = emit2(code, c, 30, 0);   // RGET the tag word
                }
                c = emit2(code, c, 1, a.nv[arm]);
                c = emit1(code, c, 10);    // EQ
                hole := c + 1;
                c = emit2(code, c, 13, 0); // JZ to the next arm
                if a.na[arm * 4] > -1 {    // bind the payload first:
                    c = emit1(code, c, 32);      // DUP the record,
                    c = emit2(code, c, 30, 1);   // RGET word 1,
                    c = emit2(code, c, 12, a.na[arm * 4]);   // STORE
                }
                c = emit1(code, c, 33);    // POP the scrutinee, run the arm
                c = emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.nl[arm]);
                eh := c + 1;
                c = emit2(code, c, 14, 0); // JMP past the rest
                if ne == 0 { ends0 = eh; }
                if ne == 1 { ends1 = eh; }
                if ne == 2 { ends2 = eh; }
                ne += 1;
                code[hole] = c;
            }
            if a.nr[arm] == -1 {           // the last arm: just take it
                if a.na[arm * 4] > -1 {
                    c = emit1(code, c, 32);
                    c = emit2(code, c, 30, 1);
                    c = emit2(code, c, 12, a.na[arm * 4]);
                }
                c = emit1(code, c, 33);
                c = emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.nl[arm]);
            }
            arm = a.nr[arm];
        }
        if ne > 0 { code[ends0] = c; }
        if ne > 1 { code[ends1] = c; }
        if ne > 2 { code[ends2] = c; }
        return c;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 16 {                   // field write: value, then the
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);   // record index,
        c2 := emit2(code, c, 11, a.na[idx * 4]);   // then RSET at the slot
        return emit2(code, c2, 31, a.nv[idx]);     // the check pass resolved
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 22 {                   // call statement: the value,
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);   // then POP —
        return emit1(code, c, 33);         // made and dropped
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 23 {                   // assignment: value, then STORE
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);   // — the bind's
        return emit2(code, c, 12, a.nv[idx]);   // own two words
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 6 {                    // if / else
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        hole := c + 1;                     // the JZ's target word
        t := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, emit2(code, c, 13, 0), a.nr[idx]);
        if a.na[idx * 4] > -1 {            // else: the diamond —
            hole2 := t + 1;                // the then-arm hops past the else,
            c2 := emit2(code, t, 14, 0);
            code[hole] = c2;               // false lands at the else entry,
            e := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, c2, a.na[idx * 4]);
            code[hole2] = e;               // and the hop lands past it all
            return e;
        }
        code[hole] = t;                    // no else: skip the arm when false
        return t;
    }
    top := cp;                             // while: cond, JZ out, body, back
    c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
    hole := c + 1;
    t := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, emit2(code, c, 13, 0), a.nr[idx]);
    e := emit2(code, t, 14, top);          // back to the condition
    code[hole] = e;                        // exit lands past the back-jump
    e
}

// Build one body — { stmt } cmp — as a single fresh tree (the node
// cursor restarts at zero: trees are transient per body now, not per
// expression), then run THE PASSES over it in order: check (seeded
// with nseed already-bound names sitting in bv), fold, emit. This is
// the compiler's whole shape in eight lines — ncc's parse → check →
// gen, at toy scale, shared by function bodies and the main program.
fn gen_body(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, env: Scope, st: Strs, nseed: i64, pos: i64, code: *i64, cp: i64) -> EP {
    mut p := pos;
    mut n2 := 0;
    mut head := -1;
    mut prev := -1;
    mut more := true;
    while more {                           // the statement chain, if any
        more = false;
        if is_stmt_start(k, v, p) {
            s := ast_stmt(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, p, n2);
            seq := s.nn;
            a.nk[seq] = 8; a.nl[seq] = s.root; a.nr[seq] = -1; a.nv[seq] = 0;
            a.nline[seq] = a.nline[s.root];
            if prev > -1 { a.nr[prev] = seq; } else { head = seq; }
            prev = seq;
            n2 = s.nn + 1;
            p = s.pos;
            more = true;
        }
    }
    if k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 125 {          // no tail before '}': a VOID
        b := check_stmt(a, sd, env, nseed, head);   // body (countdown's
        fold_stmt(a, st, head);            // put) — statements only, and
        c := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, cp, head);  // its value is 0: the
        return EP{ pos: p, cp: emit2(code, c, 1, 0) };   // toy's one kind
    }                                      // rides, stated asterisk (b
                                           // holds the checked count)
    t := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, sd, a, p, n2); // the tail is the body's value
    b := check_stmt(a, sd, env, nseed, head);  // pass 1: check
    check_node(a, sd, env, b, t.root);
    if a.nt[t.root] != 0 {                 // values and returns stay int —
        fail(a.nline[t.root], "a body's value must be an integer");
    }
    fold_stmt(a, st, head);                    // pass 2: fold
    fold_node(a, st, t.root);
    c := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, cp, head);   // pass 3: emit
    EP{ pos: t.pos, cp: emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, c, t.root) }
}

// fndef := 'fn' name '(' param { ',' param } ')' '{' { stmt } cmp '}'
// param := name [ ':' struct-name ]
//   The entry address is recorded BEFORE the body compiles, so a
//   function may call itself; up to four comma-separated parameters
//   record their slots in fp[f*4 + j] with the count in fpc[f]; the
//   body ends in RET, which returns its tail value.
//   A body's scope starts FRESH: its parameters are seeded into bv as
//   the first bound names (index 0 up — all fndefs precede the main
//   statements, so nothing is trampled), and everything else it reads
//   must be bound by its own statements. The flat runtime table would
//   let a body read main's names, but that is caller-save's private
//   business — the checker enforces the lexical story.
fn parse_fndef(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, env: Scope, sy: Syms, st: Strs, pos: i64, code: *i64, cp: i64) -> EP {
    f := v[pos + 1];
    fe[f] = cp;
    mut p := pos + 3;                      // fn +0, name +1, '(' +2, params...
    mut j := 0;
    mut going := true;
    while going {
        going = false;
        if k[p] == 1 {
            pn := v[p];
            p += 1;
            mut pt := 0;                   // unannotated = integer;
            if k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 58 && k[p + 1] == 22 {
                p += 2;                    // ': i64' and its builtin kin:
            }                              // all map to the toy's int
            if k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 58 {   // ': Name' declares a STRUCT
                if k[p + 1] != 1 {         // or ENUM parameter (N's own
                    fail(ln[p + 1], "expected a type name");
                }                          // fn f(p: P) shape)
                mut tsid := -1;
                mut si := 0;
                while si < sd.cur[0] {     // a struct name ...
                    if sd.sn[si] == v[p + 1] { tsid = si; }
                    si += 1;
                }
                if tsid >= 0 { pt = 2 + tsid; }
                mut tei := -1;
                mut ei := 0;
                while ei < sd.cur[2] {     // ... or an enum name
                    if sd.en[ei] == v[p + 1] { tei = ei; }
                    ei += 1;
                }
                if tei >= 0 { pt = 100 + tei; }
                if tsid < 0 && tei < 0 {
                    if !spells_str(sy, v[p + 1]) {
                        fail(ln[p + 1], "unknown type name");
                    }
                    pt = 1;            // ': str' — the toy's second
                }                      // kind joins the annotations
                p += 2;
            }
            fp[f * 4 + j] = pn;
            sd.fpt[f * 4 + j] = pt;        // the declared type, for call
            env.bv[j] = pn;                // sites to be held to — and the
            env.vt[pn] = pt;               // body sees the param AS its
            env.mv[pn] = 0;                // type — and immutable (N)
            j += 1;                        // type: field reads and writes
            if k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 44 {   // on a struct param just work
                p += 1;
                going = true;
            }
        }
    }
    fpc[f] = j;
    mut p2 := p + 1;                       // past ')'; an optional
    if k[p2] == 2 && v[p2] == 45 && k[p2 + 1] == 2 && v[p2 + 1] == 62 {
        if !(k[p2 + 2] == 22 || k[p2 + 2] == 1) {   // '-> Name' return
            fail(ln[p2 + 2], "expected a type name");   // annotation is
        }                                  // accepted - and every body
        p2 += 3;                           // is held to the toy's one
    }                                      // return kind, int (the tail
    t := gen_body(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, sd, a, env, st, j, p2 + 1, code, cp);
    EP{ pos: t.pos + 1, cp: emit1(code, t.cp, 16) }   // skip '}', RET
}

// Does symbol s spell "main"? The byte-compare rung 6 stands on: no
// keyword, no lexer special case — just the interned name's bytes.
fn spells_main(sy: Syms, s: i64) -> bool {
    sy.sl[s] == 4
        && sy.nb[sy.ss[s]] as i64 == 109
        && sy.nb[sy.ss[s] + 1] as i64 == 97
        && sy.nb[sy.ss[s] + 2] as i64 == 105
        && sy.nb[sy.ss[s] + 3] as i64 == 110
}

// And does it spell "str"? Same move, for parameter annotations: the
// toy's second kind is not a builtin-type token (kind 22 is the
// NUMERIC family), so ': str' arrives as a plain name and is resolved
// here by its bytes.
fn spells_str(sy: Syms, s: i64) -> bool {
    sy.sl[s] == 3
        && sy.nb[sy.ss[s]] as i64 == 115
        && sy.nb[sy.ss[s] + 1] as i64 == 116
        && sy.nb[sy.ss[s] + 2] as i64 == 114
}

// program := { fndef } { stmt } cmp  |  { fndef }
//   If any functions exist, a JMP with a placeholder target is emitted
//   first and patched to hop over their bodies — execution starts at
//   the main statements, functions are entered only by CALL. The
//   second form is rung 6, hello.n's own: when the tokens END right
//   after the fndefs and one of them is named main, there is no tail
//   to demand — the program IS one call to main, and the two words
//   emitted are exactly what a 0-argument call site would emit.
fn parse_program(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, sd: Sdefs, a: Ast, env: Scope, sy: Syms, st: Strs, pos: i64, code: *i64, cp: i64) -> EP {
    mut p := pos;
    mut c := cp;
    sd.cur[0] = 0;                         // struct defs, records, enum
    sd.cur[1] = 0;                         // defs, and extern bindings
    sd.cur[2] = 0;                         // are per-program, like the
    sd.cur[3] = 0;                         // strings
    mut sds := true;
    while sds {                            // struct declarations lead the
        sds = false;                       // program: name, then up to four
        if k[p] == 19 {                    // field names in braces — no
            if k[p + 1] != 1 {             // code, just tables
                fail(ln[p + 1], "expected a struct name");
            }
            sid := sd.cur[0];
            sd.sn[sid] = v[p + 1];
            mut q := p + 2;
            if !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 123) {
                fail(ln[q], "expected '\{' after the struct name");
            }
            q += 1;
            mut fc := 0;
            mut more := !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 125);
            while more {
                more = false;
                if k[q] != 1 { fail(ln[q], "expected a field name"); }
                if fc == 4 { fail(ln[q], "too many fields"); }
                sd.sf[sid * 4 + fc] = v[q];
                fc += 1;
                q += 1;
                if k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 44 { q += 1; more = true; }
            }
            if !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 125) {
                fail(ln[q], "expected '\}' after the fields");
            }
            sd.sfc[sid] = fc;
            sd.cur[0] = sid + 1;
            p = q + 1;
            sds = true;
        }
        if k[p] == 20 {                    // enum Name { v1, v2, ... } —
            if k[p + 1] != 1 {             // the same shape as a struct
                fail(ln[p + 1], "expected an enum name");
            }                              // decl: tables, no code; a
            eid := sd.cur[2];              // variant IS its index
            sd.en[eid] = v[p + 1];
            mut q := p + 2;
            if !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 123) {
                fail(ln[q], "expected '\{' after the enum name");
            }
            q += 1;
            mut vc := 0;
            mut more := !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 125);
            while more {
                more = false;
                if k[q] != 1 { fail(ln[q], "expected a variant name"); }
                if vc == 4 { fail(ln[q], "too many variants"); }
                sd.ev[eid * 4 + vc] = v[q];
                sd.ep[eid * 4 + vc] = 0;
                q += 1;
                if k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 40 {   // '(name)': a PAYLOAD
                    if k[q + 1] != 1 {         // variant - the name is
                        fail(ln[q + 1], "expected a payload name");
                    }                          // documentary, the payload
                    if !(k[q + 2] == 2 && v[q + 2] == 41) {   // one int
                        fail(ln[q + 2], "expected ')' after the payload");
                    }
                    sd.ep[eid * 4 + vc] = 1;
                    q += 3;
                }
                vc += 1;
                if k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 44 { q += 1; more = true; }
            }
            if !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 125) {
                fail(ln[q], "expected '\}' after the variants");
            }
            sd.evc[eid] = vc;
            sd.cur[2] = eid + 1;
            p = q + 1;
            sds = true;
        }
        if k[p] == 25 {                    // extern syscall { fn name(...)
            mut q := p + 1;                // -> type = NUMBER ... } - pure
            if k[q] == 1 { q += 1; }       // tables ('syscall' reads as an
            if !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 123) {   // identifier here)
                fail(ln[q], "expected '\{' after extern");
            }
            q += 1;
            mut more := k[q] == 7;
            while more {
                more = false;
                if k[q + 1] != 1 { fail(ln[q + 1], "expected a name"); }
                xi := sd.cur[3];
                sd.xn[xi] = v[q + 1];
                q += 2;
                if !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 40) {
                    fail(ln[q], "expected '(' after the name");
                }
                q += 1;
                mut pc2 := 0;
                mut inp := !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 41);
                while inp {                // params: name [: type tokens]
                    inp = false;           // - documentary only, so ANY
                    if !(k[q] == 1 || k[q] == 15 || k[q] == 24 || k[q] == 22) {
                        fail(ln[q], "expected a parameter name");
                    }                      // word serves (hello.n names one
                    q += 1;                // 'len', the toy's own keyword)
                    if k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 58 {
                        q += 1;            // the type: attribute prefixes
                        while k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 42 { q += 1; }
                        if !(k[q] == 22 || k[q] == 1) {
                            fail(ln[q], "expected a type name");
                        }
                        q += 1;
                    }
                    pc2 += 1;
                    if k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 44 { q += 1; inp = true; }
                }
                if !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 41) {
                    fail(ln[q], "expected ')'");
                }
                q += 1;
                if k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 45 && k[q + 1] == 2 && v[q + 1] == 62 {
                    q += 2;                // -> type, read and recorded as
                    while k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 42 { q += 1; }   // the toy's
                    if !(k[q] == 22 || k[q] == 1) {             // one kind
                        fail(ln[q], "expected a type name");
                    }
                    q += 1;
                }
                if !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 61) {
                    fail(ln[q], "expected '=' and the syscall number");
                }
                if k[q + 1] != 0 { fail(ln[q + 1], "expected the syscall number"); }
                sd.xnum[xi] = v[q + 1];
                sd.xpc[xi] = pc2;
                sd.cur[3] = xi + 1;
                q += 2;
                if k[q] == 7 { more = true; }
            }
            if !(k[q] == 2 && v[q] == 125) {
                fail(ln[q], "expected '\}' after the extern block");
            }
            p = q + 1;
            sds = true;
        }
    }
    mut msym := -1;                        // rung 6: remember main
    if k[p] == 7 {
        c = emit2(code, c, 14, 0);
        hole := c - 1;                     // the hop-over JMP's target
        mut fns := true;
        while fns {
            fns = false;
            if k[p] == 7 {
                if spells_main(sy, v[p + 1]) { msym = v[p + 1]; }
                r := parse_fndef(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, sd, a, env, sy, st, p, code, c);
                p = r.pos;
                c = r.cp;
                fns = true;
            }
        }
        code[hole] = c;                    // main statements start here
    }
    if k[p] == 9 {                         // no top-level statements at
        if msym > -1 {                     // all: the program IS one call
            if fpc[msym] != 0 {            // to main (rung 6, N's form) —
                fail(ln[p], "main takes no parameters");
            }                              // the two words a real 0-arg
            return EP{ pos: p, cp: emit2(code, c, 15, fe[msym]) };
        }                                  // call site would emit
        fail(ln[p], "a program needs statements or a main");
    }
    t := gen_body(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, sd, a, env, st, 0, p, code, c);
    if k[t.pos] != 9 {                     // main's scope starts empty
        fail(ln[t.pos], "unexpected input after the tail expression");
    }
    t
}

// The stack machine: arithmetic, comparisons, a variable table, jumps,
// calls — and output. CALL pushes the return address and jumps; RET
// consumes it beneath the return value; SWAP serves the caller-save
// restore. The string table (sb/sts/stl) rides along so PRINTS can
// reach its bytes.
fn run(prog: *i64, stk: *i64, vars: *i64, st: Strs, sd: Sdefs) -> i64 {
    mut pc := 0;
    mut sp := 0;
    mut running := true;
    while running {
        op := prog[pc];
        if op == 0 { running = false; }
        if op == 1 { stk[sp] = prog[pc + 1]; sp += 1; pc += 2; }
        if op == 2 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] + stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 3 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] - stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 4 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] * stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 5 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] / stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 6 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] % stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 7 { stk[sp - 1] = -stk[sp - 1]; pc += 1; }
        if op == 8 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] < stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 9 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] > stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 10 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] == stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 11 { stk[sp] = vars[prog[pc + 1]]; sp += 1; pc += 2; }
        if op == 12 { sp -= 1; vars[prog[pc + 1]] = stk[sp]; pc += 2; }
        if op == 13 {
            sp -= 1;
            if stk[sp] == 0 { pc = prog[pc + 1]; } else { pc += 2; }
        }
        if op == 14 { pc = prog[pc + 1]; }
        if op == 15 { stk[sp] = pc + 2; sp += 1; pc = prog[pc + 1]; }
        if op == 16 {
            rv := stk[sp - 1];             // the body's tail value
            pc = stk[sp - 2];              // the pushed return address
            stk[sp - 2] = rv;
            sp -= 1;
        }
        if op == 17 {
            t := stk[sp - 1];
            stk[sp - 1] = stk[sp - 2];
            stk[sp - 2] = t;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 18 {                      // PRINT: the popped number
            sp -= 1;
            x := stk[sp];
            put("{x}\n");
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 19 {                      // PRINTS: pop a table index and
            sp -= 1;                       // write that entry — strings are
            s := stk[sp];                  // stack values like any other
            bput(st.sb, st.sts[s], st.stl[s]);
            put("\n");
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 20 {                      // STREQ: pop two table indexes,
            y := stk[sp - 1];              // byte-compare the spans
            x := stk[sp - 2];
            mut eq := 0;
            if st.stl[x] == st.stl[y] {
                eq = 1;
                mut q := 0;
                while q < st.stl[x] {
                    if st.sb[st.sts[x] + q] as i64 != st.sb[st.sts[y] + q] as i64 {
                        eq = 0;
                    }
                    q += 1;
                }
            }
            stk[sp - 2] = eq;
            sp -= 1;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 21 {                      // CONCAT: pop two indexes, append
            y := stk[sp - 1];              // a fresh entry = x's bytes then
            x := stk[sp - 2];              // y's, push the new index
            base := st.cur[1];
            mut q := 0;
            while q < st.stl[x] {
                st.sb[base + q] = st.sb[st.sts[x] + q];
                q += 1;
            }
            mut w := 0;
            while w < st.stl[y] {
                st.sb[base + st.stl[x] + w] = st.sb[st.sts[y] + w];
                w += 1;
            }
            ni := st.cur[0];
            st.sts[ni] = base;
            st.stl[ni] = st.stl[x] + st.stl[y];
            st.cur[0] = ni + 1;
            st.cur[1] = base + st.stl[ni];
            stk[sp - 2] = ni;
            sp -= 1;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 22 {                      // NEQ / LE / GE: the rest of the
            mut t := 0;                    // comparison set, LT/GT/EQ-shaped
            if stk[sp - 2] != stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 23 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] <= stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 24 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] >= stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 25 {                      // STRLEN: a table index becomes
            stk[sp - 1] = st.stl[stk[sp - 1]];   // its span's length
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 26 {                      // NOT: zero flips to one and
            mut t := 0;                    // anything else to zero
            if stk[sp - 1] == 0 { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 1] = t;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 27 {                      // STRNEQ: STREQ's byte-compare,
            y := stk[sp - 1];              // pushing the flip
            x := stk[sp - 2];
            mut eq := 0;
            if st.stl[x] == st.stl[y] {
                eq = 1;
                mut q := 0;
                while q < st.stl[x] {
                    if st.sb[st.sts[x] + q] as i64 != st.sb[st.sts[y] + q] as i64 {
                        eq = 0;
                    }
                    q += 1;
                }
            }
            stk[sp - 2] = 1 - eq;
            sp -= 1;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 28 {                      // ITOS: pop an integer, append
            mut x := stk[sp - 1];          // its decimal image as a fresh
            base := st.cur[1];             // entry, push the entry's index
            mut len := 0;
            if x < 0 {
                st.sb[base] = 45 as u8;
                len = 1;
                x = -x;
            }
            mut m := 1;
            while x / m >= 10 { m = m * 10; }
            while m > 0 {
                st.sb[base + len] = (48 + (x / m) % 10) as u8;
                len += 1;
                m = m / 10;
            }
            ni := st.cur[0];
            st.sts[ni] = base;
            st.stl[ni] = len;
            st.cur[0] = ni + 1;
            st.cur[1] = base + len;
            stk[sp - 1] = ni;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 29 {                      // RNEW k: pop k field values into
            cnt := prog[pc + 1];           // a fresh record (first field
            base := sd.cur[1];             // lowest), push the record's base
            mut q := 0;                    // index — a struct at run time IS
            while q < cnt {                // its index, like a string
                sd.rw[base + q] = stk[sp - cnt + q];
                q += 1;
            }
            sd.cur[1] = base + cnt;
            sp -= cnt;
            stk[sp] = base;
            sp += 1;
            pc += 2;
        }
        if op == 30 {                      // RGET off: replace the record
            stk[sp - 1] = sd.rw[stk[sp - 1] + prog[pc + 1]];   // index with
            pc += 2;                       // the field's word
        }
        if op == 32 {                      // DUP: the match scrutinee
            stk[sp] = stk[sp - 1];         // stays put while arms test it
            sp += 1;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 33 {                      // POP: an arm matched, the tag
            sp -= 1;                       // has served
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 34 {                      // SYSCALL xi: serviced by the
            xi := prog[pc + 1];            // binding's NUMBER. write (1)
            num := sd.xnum[xi];            // pops len/index/fd and writes
            mut res := 0 - 1;              // the span through the same
            if num == 1 {                  // audited boundary PRINTS uses;
                len2 := stk[sp - 1];       // getpid (6) is a constant; any
                si2 := stk[sp - 2];        // other number answers -1, the
                bput(st.sb, st.sts[si2], len2);   // host shim's own rule
                res = len2;
            }
            if num == 6 { res = 7; }
            sp -= sd.xpc[xi];
            stk[sp] = res;
            sp += 1;
            pc += 2;
        }
        if op == 31 {                      // RSET off: pop the record index
            idx2 := stk[sp - 1];           // then the value, store the
            val2 := stk[sp - 2];           // field's word in place
            sd.rw[idx2 + prog[pc + 1]] = val2;
            sp -= 2;
            pc += 2;
        }
    }
    stk[0]
}

// The whole pipeline in one call: tokens in, code out, value from the VM.
fn show(src: str, k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, code: *i64, stk: *i64, vars: *i64,
        fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, env: Scope, sy: Syms, st: Strs,
        sd: Sdefs) {
    lex(src.ptr as *u8, src.len as i64, k, v, ln, sy, st);
    r := parse_program(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, sd, a, env, sy, st, 0, code, 0);
    words := emit1(code, r.cp, 0);         // HALT closes the program
    put("{src}\n");                        // echo first: a program's prints
    val := run(code, stk, vars, st, sd);       // land under its source
    put("  ->  {val}   [{words} code words]\n");
}

// The selfhost optic, at toy scale: compile and run a program this
// binary does NOT embed. The audited wrapper owns every kernel
// crossing (open/sbrk/read/close); the same single lexer that eats
// the embedded demos eats the file's bytes, because it never wanted a
// str in the first place — just bytes and a length. Missing file =
// a graceful skip (the fsio pattern; in-OS the file comes from /mnt).
#[caps(syscall)]
fn run_file(path: str, k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, code: *i64, stk: *i64, vars: *i64,
            fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, env: Scope, sy: Syms, st: Strs,
            sd: Sdefs) {
    fd := sys_open(path.ptr as *u8, 0, 0);
    if fd < 0 {
        put("(no toy demo file - file demo skipped)\n");
        return;
    }
    buf := sys_sbrk(1024) as *u8;
    len := sys_read(fd, buf, 1024);
    sys_close(fd);
    if len < 1 {
        put("(no toy demo file - file demo skipped)\n");
        return;
    }
    put("compiling {path} from disk:\n");
    lex(buf, len, k, v, ln, sy, st);
    r := parse_program(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, sd, a, env, sy, st, 0, code, 0);
    words := emit1(code, r.cp, 0);         // HALT closes the program
    val := run(code, stk, vars, st, sd);
    put("  ->  {val}   [{words} code words]\n");
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    k := alloc_words(192);                 // countdown.n runs ~125 tokens
    v := alloc_words(192);
    code := alloc_words(256);
    stk := alloc_words(64);
    vars := alloc_words(64);               // slots are symbol indexes
    fe := alloc_words(64);
    fp := alloc_words(256);                // 64 fns x up to 4 param slots
    fpc := alloc_words(64);
    nb := alloc_bytes(256);                // the interned-names buffer
    ss := alloc_words(64);
    sl := alloc_words(64);
    sy := Syms{ nb: nb, ss: ss, sl: sl };
    ln := alloc_words(192);                // per-token source lines (rung 2)
    ast := Ast{ nk: alloc_words(96), nl: alloc_words(96), nr: alloc_words(96),
                nv: alloc_words(96), na: alloc_words(384),
                nline: alloc_words(96),
                nt: alloc_words(96) };     // the TYPE COLUMN (0 int, 1 str)
    env := Scope{ bv: alloc_words(64), vt: alloc_words(64),
                  mv: alloc_words(64) };   // per-name mut-ness
    st := Strs{ sb: alloc_bytes(256),      // the string table: bytes + spans
                sts: alloc_words(32), stl: alloc_words(32),
                cur: alloc_words(2) };     // + the shared write cursors
    sd := Sdefs{ sn: alloc_words(16), sf: alloc_words(64),
                 sfc: alloc_words(16),     // struct defs: 16 x 4 fields
                 rw: alloc_words(128),     // + the runtime record words
                 cur: alloc_words(4),      // + counts / words-used cursors
                 fpt: alloc_words(256),    // + declared parameter types
                 en: alloc_words(16), ev: alloc_words(64),
                 evc: alloc_words(16),     // + enum defs: 16 x 4 variants
                 ep: alloc_words(64),      // + per-variant payload flags
                 xn: alloc_words(16), xnum: alloc_words(16),
                 xpc: alloc_words(16) };   // + extern syscall bindings
    put("a type column over the toy - lex -> parse -> check -> fold -> emit -> run:\n");
    // Braces are escaped so they read as text, not interpolation (§2.5).
    show("fn max(a, b) \{ mut m := 0; if a > b \{ m = a; \} else \{ m = b; \} m \} max(3, 9) + max(7, 2)",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    show("fn gcd(a, b) \{ mut r := a; if b > 0 \{ r = gcd(b, a % b); \} r \} gcd(48, 18)",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    show("fn fact(n) \{ mut r := 1; if n > 1 \{ r = n * fact(n - 1); \} r \} fact(5)",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // Folding, visible: unfolded this program emits 19 code words; the
    // fold pass collapses 2*3+4 to 10 and 10-8 to 2 at compile time and
    // 10 words remain. (The flagship demos above fold nothing — their
    // constants sit alone as call arguments — so their counts held.)
    show("d := 2 * 3 + 4; // constants fold\nd * (10 - 8)",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // Dead-arm elimination, visible: the condition folds to 0, the if
    // dissolves, and 7 words remain of an unfolded 18. The dead arm was
    // still checked first — dead code must be legal code.
    show("if 1 > 2 \{ x := 999; \} y := 5; y",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // The toy speaks, and strings are VALUES: one is bound to a name,
    // loaded back, and printed — same stack, same vars table, same
    // LOAD as any integer, with the type column picking PRINTS. The
    // computed (and folded — PUSH 42) number takes PRINT instead:
    // one syntax, two opcodes, decided by the checker.
    show("greet := \"hello from the toy\"; print greet; print len(greet); print 6 * 7; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // The type column's second dividend: == over two strings resolves
    // to STREQ (and != to STRNEQ — the equality pair is symmetric) —
    // and since both yield an int, string equality composes with
    // plain conditions for free.
    show("name := \"nyx\"; if name == \"nyx\" \{ print \"the goddess\"; \} print name == \"day\"; print name != \"day\"; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // And + MAKES strings: the first print concatenates at RUN time (a
    // load meets a literal — the VM appends a fresh table entry), the
    // second at COMPILE time (two literals — the fold pass appends
    // through the same cursor and PRINTS a ready-made entry), and the
    // result chains into == like any string.
    show("who := \"nyx\"; print who + \" rules\"; print \"nyx\" + \"os\"; print who + \"os\" == \"nyxos\"; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // INTERPOLATION — the real lexer's hardest trick, in the toy: a
    // string splits at its braces (HEAD/MID/TAIL segment tokens, the
    // lexer switching to expression mode inside each hole) and the
    // parser desugars the pieces into the plain + chain the type
    // column already compiles — an int hole becomes text through ITOS,
    // a str hole inserts as itself. The second print is all-constant:
    // ITOS folds the digits, the CONCATs collapse, and the entire
    // string becomes ONE literal at compile time.
    show("n := 6 * 7; w := \"toy\";\nprint \"the \{w\} says \{n\}\";\nprint \"\{1 + 2\} and \{40 + 2\}\"; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // Number spellings, block comments, and attributes, N's own: 0x2a
    // is 42 in hex (lowercase at toy scale), underscores group digits
    // and vanish, /* block comments nest */ by depth — newlines inside
    // still ticking the line counter — and an #[attribute] lexes and
    // is deliberately ignored (surface parity; the meanings are ncc's).
    show("h := 0x2a; /* hex /* nests */ properly */ #[toy] big := 1_000;\nprint \"\{h\} and \{big\}\"; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // STRUCTS: declared, made, MUTATED, read. A record lives in a side
    // table — the value on the stack IS its base index, like a string —
    // and the type column (type ids: 2+k for struct k) is what keeps
    // it from adding like the number it secretly is. The check pass
    // resolves each field name to its slot; emit stays type-blind.
    // `p.x = e;` writes through the record (RSET), so the read after
    // it sees 8 where the literal put 3.
    show("struct P \{ x, y \}\np := P\{3, 4\};\np.x = p.x + 5;\nprint p.x * 10 + p.y; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // Structs CROSS CALLS now: `fn bump(q: P)` declares its parameter's
    // type, the call site is held to it, and the body sees q AS a P —
    // field reads and writes on a parameter just work. What crosses is
    // the record's INDEX, so the callee's write is visible in the
    // caller afterward: reference semantics, stated plainly (a string
    // crosses the same way; only the index is copied).
    show("struct P \{ x, y \}\nfn bump(q: P) \{ q.x = q.x + 1; q.y \}\np := P\{3, 4\};\nprint bump(p);\nprint p.x; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // ENUMS + MATCH: a tag-only enum (a variant IS its index, typed
    // 100+e so it cannot leak into arithmetic), and match — the ONLY
    // eliminator: arms are DUP/EQ/JZ chains, the last arm runs with no
    // test because the checker PROVED exhaustiveness (every variant
    // covered exactly once, or a located refusal). The tag never
    // prints, never adds, never interpolates: you match on it.
    show("enum Shape \{ circle, square, dot \}\ns := Shape.square;\nmatch s \{ circle \{ print 1; \} square \{ print 22; \} dot \{ print 3; \} \}\n0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // PAYLOAD variants: an enum with any `name(x)` variant makes ALL
    // its values records — tag at word 0, the payload word after it,
    // built by the same RNEW that builds structs (a bare variant is a
    // one-word record). A match arm may BIND the payload — `num(x)`
    // stores word 1 into x for that arm's chain only, and the binder
    // dies at the brace like every arm name. Tag-only enums keep
    // their bare-integer representation untouched.
    show("enum Msg \{ num(x), quit \}\nm := Msg.num(41);\nmatch m \{ num(x) \{ print x + 1; \} quit \{ print 0; \} \}\n0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // STRING ESCAPES — rung 1 of the road to parsing N itself (see
    // docs/selfhost.md, "The next mountain"): \n breaks the line
    // inside one literal, and an ESCAPED brace is a literal brace —
    // it never reaches the lexer's hole test, so interpolation and
    // braces-as-text finally coexist, exactly N's own rule.
    // (The demo source below escapes twice: once for N, once for the
    // toy — the toy sees a backslash-brace and keeps the byte.)
    show("print \"one\\ntwo\"; print \"kept: \\\{ \\\} done\"; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // N'S SIGNATURES AND CASTS — rungs 2+3 of the hello.n road: the
    // builtin type names are reserved words now, `: i64` in a
    // parameter and `-> i64` on a fn are accepted (every one maps to
    // the toy's single numeric kind — the tail check was already
    // holding bodies to it), and `as i64` reads a cast and keeps the
    // value untouched: types cannot change an i64 word, and the toy
    // says so instead of pretending.
    show("fn dbl(v: i64) -> i64 \{ v * 2 \}\nn := dbl(21) as i64 + 0;\nprint n; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // STR FIELDS + EXTERN SYSCALLS — rungs 4+5 of the hello.n road:
    // `.len` IS the len builtin (same node, opcode, folding), `.ptr`
    // is the identity (a string IS its table index — the asterisk is
    // stated), and an `extern syscall` block binds fns the VM services
    // BY NUMBER: write pushes its span through the same audited
    // boundary PRINTS uses, getpid answers 7, anything unmapped -1 —
    // the host shim's own rule. The shape is hello.n's, verbatim.
    show("extern syscall \{\nfn write(fd: i64, buf: i64, len: i64) -> i64 = 1\nfn getpid() -> i64 = 6\n\}\nmsg := \"toy syscall!\\n\";\nw := write(1, msg.ptr, msg.len);\nprint getpid(); w",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // The rest of the comparison set, each its own opcode.
    show("n := 7; print n != 7; print n <= 7; print n >= 9; print !(n >= 9); 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // else-if chains: free structure — the else slot held a statement
    // all along, and a nested if is a statement.
    show("g := 72;\nif g >= 90 \{ print \"gold\"; \} else if g >= 60 \{ print \"silver\"; \} else \{ print \"bronze\"; \}\ng",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // And a program this binary does not carry: read off the disk,
    // compiled by the same pipeline, run by the same VM. Its source is
    // examples/n_toy_demo.toy — and it uses the WHOLE surface: a fn, a
    // while-sum, a bound string, and an interpolated print whose holes
    // hold a variable, and a call — nothing about the pipeline knows
    // or cares that the bytes came from a file.
    run_file("/tmp/n_toy_demo.n",
             k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // RUNG 6, THE LADDER'S LAST: fn main as the program. The tokens
    // end right after the fndefs — no tail to demand — so the program
    // is one CALL to main, N's own top-level form. And inside the
    // body, a bare CALL is a statement now: made, then dropped (POP).
    // The scan that decides tells a call statement from a call TAIL
    // by N's own rule — the ';'.
    show("fn shout(x) \{ print x; 0 \}\nfn main() \{ shout(41 + 1); shout(43); 0 \}",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // COUNTDOWN'S FIRST RUNGS: ': str' parameter annotations (the
    // toy's second kind joins fpt — call sites held to it, the body
    // sees the param as a str) and VOID bodies (no tail before the
    // closing brace: statements only, the body's value is 0, stated)
    // — which is countdown.n's own put(s: str) shape, both at once.
    show("fn greet(who: str) \{ print who; \}\ngreet(\"toy\"); greet(\"graduate\"); 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // MUT + ASSIGNMENT — countdown's last rung, and an honest rule
    // swap: ':=' always DECLARES (immutable by default), 'mut' marks
    // a name writable, and '=' assigns — refused on an immutable name
    // with ncc's own words. The toy's flat table cannot shadow (ncc
    // can), so re-':=' of a live name refuses too — the divergence
    // stated, not papered over. The demos above already converted:
    // gcd/fact/max now mutate the way real N does.
    show("mut acc := 1;\nmut i := 1;\nwhile i <= 5 \{ acc = acc * i; i = i + 1; \}\nprint acc; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // THE GRADUATION: hello.n ITSELF — the canonical N program, off
    // the disk, verbatim: its extern syscall block, its fn main ->
    // i64, its interpolated string, its casts, its bare write(...);
    // statement. Six rungs were named on the road here (escapes,
    // signatures, casts, str fields, extern syscalls, main) and this
    // is the moment they were for: the toy compiles the program every
    // N milestone began with, and the program prints — through its
    // own write, pid and all.
    run_file("/tmp/n_hello.n",
             k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // THE SECOND GRADUATION: countdown.n — the second example ever
    // written, compiled whole off the disk: its str-parameter helper
    // put(s: str) (a void body), its mut counter assigned down the
    // while loop, an interpolated bind re-executed per iteration, and
    // a call in an interpolation hole. Five ticks, then liftoff —
    // through its own write, pid and all.
    run_file("/tmp/n_countdown.n",
             k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    // The broken program runs LAST (fail() ends the process — one failing
    // demo per run). It PARSES cleanly; what is wrong is a TYPE: a string
    // fed to +. The check pass refuses it AT the operator — line and
    // column point at the '+' itself — a
    // bug the machine could never catch (an index adds like any number).
    // Earlier failing demos rotate through this slot as new checks land;
    // the unknown-variable and expect-point refusals still stand guard,
    // all ending through the same fail().
    put("and a deliberately broken program:\n");
    show("x := 1;\nprint x + \"oops\";\nx",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, sy, st, sd);
    0
}
